    <node id="DIAGNOSTIC TESTS">
      <data key="d0">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">DIAGNOSTIC TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Diagnostic tests are procedures and tools used to diagnose or evaluate diseases, including cancer. In the context of oncology, they are used to confirm the presence of cancer, determine whether the disease is localized or advanced, and help plan appropriate treatment. These tests also play a critical role in monitoring patients after treatment, as they can detect whether cancer is still growing or spreading. Diagnostic tests include a range of methods such as imaging scans (e.g., CT, MRI, or X-rays), biopsies (removal and examination of tissue samples), and genetic or biomarker tests that analyze DNA, genes, proteins, or other molecular markers. In addition, some diagnostic procedures, such as lumbar puncture, may be used to evaluate diseases affecting the nervous system or to check for the spread of cancer into the cerebrospinal fluid. By providing detailed information about the presence, type, and extent of disease, diagnostic tests are essential for accurate diagnosis, staging, treatment planning, and ongoing surveillance to assess the effectiveness of therapy and detect any recurrence or progression.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Diagnostic tests include biopsies
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A biopsy is one type of diagnostic test used to confirm prostate cancer
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Diagnostic tests help plan treatment after a diagnosis and can monitor whether cancer is growing or spreading after treatment
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: IMAGING TESTS
  - Description: Diagnostic tests include imaging scans, biopsies, and genetic tests; imaging tests are a type of diagnostic test
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: Diagnostic tests confirm prostate cancer and determine whether it is localized or advanced
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: CT scans are diagnostic imaging tests
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: DIAGNOSIS
  - Description: Diagnostic tests are used to confirm cancer and support diagnosis
  - Source: PET SCAN
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: PET scans are diagnostic imaging tests
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: MRI scans are diagnostic imaging tests
  - Source: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A bone marrow biopsy is a diagnostic test used to confirm AML
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A bone marrow aspirate is a diagnostic test used to confirm AML
  - Source: GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: Genetic and biomarker tests are diagnostic tests used to learn about AML and guide treatment
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Diagnostic tests provide information used for tumor staging in prostate cancer
  - Source: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A lumbar puncture is a diagnostic test that checks spinal fluid for leukemia cells
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: INSURANCE
  - Description: The patient asks whether insurance will pay for the diagnostic tests they need
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: GENETIC TESTS
  - Description: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS encompass a broad category of medical assessments used to identify the presence or cause of a condition. Within this category, GENETIC TESTS are included as a specific subset. GENETIC TESTS are diagnostic tests that look for inherited mutations related to prostate cancer risk. Together, these entities describe the relationship between the broader diagnostic category and the specialized genetic testing used to assess inherited prostate cancer susceptibility.
  - Source: GENERAL HEALTH HISTORY
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: The general health history is used along with diagnostic tests to guide prostate cancer care</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
