    <node id="TREATMENT">
      <data key="d0">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d1">TREATMENT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">TREATMENT refers to the broad range of medical care and interventions provided to cancer patients after diagnosis. It encompasses standard-of-care therapies such as surgery, radiation therapy, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, systemic therapy, endocrine therapy, stem cell or hematopoietic cell transplant, and participation in clinical trials, as well as supportive care. The primary goals of treatment are to cure cancer, put it into remission, slow disease progression, reduce symptoms, and help people live longer. Treatment is planned and delivered by the care team based on a thorough risk assessment, the extent of cancer, and diagnostic testing. Tests such as blood tests, bone marrow aspirate and biopsy, and imaging studies are used to plan treatment, evaluate how well treatment is working, and monitor a patient's health after treatment ends. Imaging may also be used before, during, or after treatment to help plan care, assess response, and check for recurrence. For lower-risk cases—particularly in prostate cancer and basal cell skin cancer—treatment may be avoided or delayed, with some patients requiring minimal or no intervention. Conversely, higher-risk or more aggressive disease usually requires more intensive treatment. Treatment plans are often delivered in phases, as seen in acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where systemic therapy, chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and hematopoietic cell transplant are administered in stages with the goal of achieving remission. The approach for cancers such as cervical cancer may include surgery, radiation therapy, chemoradiation, systemic therapy, and clinical trials, depending on how far the cancer has spread. Bladder cancer treatment may be stronger or more intense than other options, while prostate cancer management focuses on slowing the disease, reducing symptoms, and prolonging survival. Regardless of cancer type, treatment can have significant side effects, including effects on fertility, hearing, endocrine glands, and a developing baby. The potential for toxicity and long-term outcomes are important considerations when selecting a treatment plan. Overall, treatment is the comprehensive management of cancer, integrating therapeutic procedures, supportive care, and ongoing monitoring to optimize patient outcomes throughout the cancer journey.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Diagnostic tests help plan treatment after a diagnosis and can monitor whether cancer is growing or spreading after treatment</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
