    <node id="BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Biopsy is a diagnostic medical procedure that removes a sample of tissue or fluid from the body for laboratory testing and pathology review. It is used to detect cancer cells, confirm a cancer diagnosis, and determine a tumor’s type, grade, and stage. The sample is examined under a microscope by a pathologist and may also be tested for biomarkers such as estrogen receptor (ER), progesterone receptor (PR), human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2), and tumor grade. Imaging techniques such as ultrasound, CT, or endorectal ultrasound may be used before, during, or after the procedure to guide the biopsy or assess the sampled area.

Biopsy can be performed using various methods, including fine-needle aspiration (FNA), fine-needle biopsy, core needle biopsy, surgical open biopsy, and bone marrow biopsy. It may be done during other procedures such as cystoscopy or initial surgery. In prostate cancer detection, a prostate biopsy is an invasive procedure typically performed by a urologist, in which 12 or more core samples are taken from different parts of the prostate using a hollow needle; samples are examined for cancer cells and assigned Gleason patterns. This biopsy may be needed after abnormal PSA test or digital rectal exam results, and may be repeated under active surveillance.

Biopsy is used across many cancer types. For ovarian cancer, it confirms the tumor before starting chemotherapy and determines tumor type and grade. For liver cancer, a needle biopsy can obtain a core of tissue from a liver tumor or metastasis. For bone cancer, biopsy types include core needle and surgical open biopsy. It is also used to diagnose anal cancer, esophageal cancer, basal cell skin cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer, and ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS), and to sample suspicious lymph nodes for cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) spread. Biopsy can be part of clinical staging, and its results—along with imaging studies—help determine the patient’s treatment plan. The decision to perform a biopsy may depend on nodule size and ultrasound features, and in some cases biopsy is performed when follow-up examination suggests cancer progression. These tissue-sampling procedures are considered essential for an accurate tumor diagnosis and can guide subsequent treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: DIAGNOSING ESOPHAGEAL CANCER
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsy is used to obtain tissue for confirming esophageal cancer
  - Source: TESTING FOR OVARIAN CANCER
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: Biopsy is a key diagnostic test to remove tissue and determine the type of ovarian cancer.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGING TESTS">
      <data key="d0">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Imaging tests are diagnostic procedures that create pictures of the inside of the body to evaluate cancer. They are used across many cancer types to diagnose, stage, and guide treatment. The tests can reveal the presence, size, location, and shape of a primary tumor, and determine whether cancer has spread to nearby tissues, organs, lymph nodes, or distant parts of the body. This information is essential for clinical staging, assessing tumor spread, and planning treatment.

Common imaging modalities include X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), FDG-PET/CT, ultrasound, mammograms, and bone scans. Some tests, such as transrectal ultrasound or MRI, are used specifically to see if cancer has spread beyond the prostate, and may not be needed for early-stage prostate cancer. Mammograms create pictures of the breast and are used in evaluating breast cancer and determining treatment planning. Ultrasound can be used to diagnose and confirm liver cancer. CT, MRI, and FDG-PET/CT are used to image tumors of the head and neck, such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma, helping determine stage and treatment plan. Imaging tests also play a role in ovarian cancer by showing tumor location, size, and shape, and whether it has spread beyond the ovaries before surgery. For bladder cancer, scans show whether the cancer has spread to nearby tissues, organs, lymph nodes, or distant parts of the body. For esophageal cancer, imaging tests are used to diagnose and determine the extent of the disease. In leukemia, imaging tests such as CT, MRI, and PET scans are used to look for leukemia outside the bone marrow, as well as areas of infection or bleeding.

Imaging tests are used during the initial workup and also during follow-up and surveillance to evaluate whether cancer has returned. A radiologist typically interprets the images and sends a report to the patient's doctor. Some imaging tests require contrast material and specific preparation. Overall, these tests are indispensable in cancer care for detecting cancer deposits, determining the extent of disease, and informing treatment decisions. By taking pictures of the inside of the body, imaging tests help the care team stage cancer, plan treatment, and monitor patients over time. They are performed in a variety of clinical settings, from initial diagnosis through long-term follow-up, and are adapted to the specific cancer type and clinical question. Whether assessing a primary tumor, checking for metastasis, or confirming a diagnosis, imaging tests provide critical visual information that guides medical decision-making. Their results are used in treatment planning for many cancers, including breast, nasopharyngeal, ovarian, bladder, esophageal, liver, and prostate cancers. In summary, imaging tests are a cornerstone of modern oncology, enabling precise evaluation of cancer and its spread.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity beyond those already captured)</data>
    </node>
