    <node id="CT SCAN">
      <data key="d0">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A computed tomography (CT or CAT) scan is an imaging procedure that uses x-rays and computer technology to create detailed pictures of the inside of the body. It takes many x-ray images of the same body part from different angles and combines them into cross-sectional, three-dimensional images, providing more detail than a standard x-ray. The procedure is faster than MRI and is often used when MRI is unavailable or not recommended. Contrast material—which may be injected or swallowed—is frequently used to make tissues and organs show up more clearly, though contrast is not always needed.

CT scans have a wide range of clinical applications. They are used to help diagnose cancers, including primary CNS lymphoma, and to determine whether cancers such as ovarian, bladder, or colon cancer have spread to other organs. For example, a CT of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis can show whether ovarian cancer has spread outside the ovaries or whether lymph nodes are enlarged. CT of the chest and abdomen may be part of initial testing to see if cancer has spread to the liver, lungs, or other organs. In colon cancer, CT is used for surveillance in stage 2 and 3 disease and when recurrence is suspected. CT can also be used to guide procedures such as lymph node biopsy, and during radiation therapy simulation it obtains pictures of the cancer site to help plan treatment. Additionally, CT is often combined with positron emission tomography (PET) to create a PET/CT scan, which offers more detailed imaging than either test alone.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING TEST
  - Target: CT SCAN
  - Description: CT is a type of imaging test.
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: IMAGING TEST
  - Description: CT scan is a type of imaging test.</data>
    </node>
