      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">(No standalone entity description provided in the data tables for PET SCAN; however, it is referenced as an imaging test.)
**Relationships:**
  - Source: RADIOLOGIST
  - Target: PET SCAN
  - Description: A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting imaging tests, including positron emission tomography (PET) scans. The radiologist reads and analyzes PET images to provide diagnostic information that helps guide patient care. PET scans are a form of nuclear medicine imaging that produce detailed images of functional processes in the body, and the radiologist plays a key role in interpreting these images to identify abnormalities and support clinical decisions. 

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI SCAN">
      <data key="d0">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging procedure that uses radio waves and powerful magnets to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body without the use of x-rays or ionizing radiation. It is commonly used to evaluate the prostate gland, including measuring its size, assessing prostate cancer, checking whether cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes or pelvic bones, planning treatment, and monitoring for recurrence. The scan can be performed before or after a prostate biopsy, and may be combined with ultrasound during a biopsy to improve accuracy. It can also be combined with positron emission tomography in a PET/MRI scan, producing highly precise images. Contrast material is often used to enhance image quality. MRI scans take longer than CT scans and are available in closed and open forms; closed MRI machines may cause claustrophobia in some patients.
Relationships:
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: TECHNOLOGIST
  - Description: The technologist operates the MRI machine and should be told about any metal in the body
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: CLOSED MRI
  - Description: Closed MRI is a type of MRI machine with an enclosed capsule-like design
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: OPEN MRI
  - Description: Open MRI is a type of MRI machine with a magnetic top and bottom and openings at each end
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: MRI scans may be combined with ultrasound to guide a prostate biopsy.
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: MRI may be used before a biopsy to target suspicious areas, during biopsy to guide removal, or after biopsy to see cancer size and location.
  - Source: CARE TEAM
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: Patients should talk to their care team about claustrophobia before closed MRI; contrast is also often used for MRI.
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: IMAGING TESTS
  - Description: MRI scan is a type of imaging test
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: Imaging tests include the MRI scan, which is one of the imaging methods described in the text. The MRI scan is specifically used as an imaging method for prostate cancer, and it is also utilized to measure prostate size.
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: CT SCAN
  - Description: CT scan is used instead when MRI is unavailable or not recommended.
  - Source: CT SCAN
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: CT scans and MRI scans are both medical imaging tests used to visualize internal structures of the body, but they differ in several key aspects. MRI scans take longer to perform than CT scans, making the overall duration of an MRI exam generally more time-consuming. Additionally, the types of contrast material used for CT and MRI are different; CT scans typically use iodine-based contrast agents, whereas MRI scans commonly use gadolinium-based contrast agents. These differences in contrast materials reflect the distinct imaging technologies and the specific diagnostic information each scan provides.
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: PET SCAN
  - Description: MRI SCAN (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) and PET SCAN (Positron Emission Tomography) are two distinct imaging methods that can be combined into a single PET/MRI scanning procedure. In this combined approach, the PET scan and MRI scan work together to provide a more detailed and comprehensive image than either modality could offer alone. The PET component is particularly useful for identifying areas of abnormal metabolic activity, which helps determine where cancer cells are located. Meanwhile, the MRI component supplies high-resolution anatomical detail of soft tissues. By integrating both technologies, PET/MRI scanning enables clinicians to correlate functional information from the PET scan with structural information from the MRI scan, leading to more accurate diagnosis and localization of disease. Thus, MRI SCAN and PET SCAN are complementary imaging tools, and their combined use in PET/MRI scanning enhances the ability to pinpoint cancerous sites and assess the extent of disease.
  - Source: RADIOLOGIST
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in reading and interpreting imaging tests, including MRI scans. The radiologist examines MRI images to provide diagnostic information that helps guide patient care.
  - Source: IMAGING TEST
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: MRI is a type of imaging test.
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: BRAIN MRI SCAN
  - Description: A brain MRI scan is an MRI scan of the brain
  - Source: BRAIN MRI SCAN
  - Target: MRI (MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING) SCAN
  - Description: A brain MRI scan is an MRI scan focused on the brain
  - Source: MRI SCAN
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: MRI scans can help plan treatment and are used after treatment to check for recurrence
  - Source: DOCTOR
  - Target: MRI SCAN
  - Description: The doctor reviews MRI results and discusses the next steps of care with the patient.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BONE MARROW BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">BONE MARROW BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">BONE MARROW BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Bone marrow biopsy is a diagnostic procedure that involves the removal of a sample of bone marrow tissue for testing. It is typically performed as a bedside procedure using a wider needle to extract a core sample of bone marrow. The sample is often taken from the back of the hip bone. This procedure may cause temporary bone pain and bruising. A bone marrow biopsy is frequently performed at the same time as a bone marrow aspirate. It is used to confirm a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and to evaluate bone marrow health for various other conditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: Bone marrow aspirate and bone marrow biopsy are two types of bone marrow tests that are often performed together as bedside procedures. Both are procedures used to remove a sample of bone marrow tissue or cells for testing. In the diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), these two tests are used together to confirm the diagnosis. They are commonly done at the same time to provide complementary information: the aspirate collects bone marrow cells, while the biopsy removes a small core of bone marrow tissue. Together, they allow for a comprehensive evaluation of the bone marrow.
  - Source: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: A bone marrow biopsy is a type of biopsy that removes a sample of bone marrow tissue or cells for testing.
  - Source: ACUTE MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: Bone marrow biopsy is used to confirm a diagnosis of AML.
  - Source: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A bone marrow biopsy is a diagnostic test used to confirm AML.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: NCCN patient guidelines describe bone marrow biopsy as a diagnostic procedure used for AML evaluation.
  - Source: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Target: DIAGNOSIS
  - Description: Bone marrow biopsy is used to confirm a diagnosis of AML.
  - Source: BONE MARROW TESTS
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: Bone marrow biopsy is one type of bone marrow test.
  - Source: PATHOLOGIST
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: A pathologist analyzes tissue removed during a biopsy, such as a bone marrow biopsy.
  - Source: HEMATOPATHOLOGIST
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: A hematopathologist analyzes cells and tissues removed during a biopsy, including a bone marrow biopsy.
  - Source: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Target: IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY
  - Description: Immunohistochemistry is one of the tests that may be performed on biopsied bone marrow cells to help diagnose leukemia.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BONE MARROW ASPIRATE">
      <data key="d0">BONE MARROW ASPIRATE</data>
      <data key="d1">BONE MARROW ASPIRATE</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Bone marrow aspirate is a diagnostic procedure in which a sample of liquid bone marrow tissue or cells is removed for testing. It is typically performed as a bedside procedure using a hollow needle, which is inserted into the back of the hip bone (pelvis) to draw liquid bone marrow into a syringe. This sample is then sent for laboratory analysis to aid in diagnosis. The procedure is commonly used to confirm a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) and other bone marrow-related conditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: BONE MARROW BIOPSY
  - Description: Bone marrow aspirate and bone marrow biopsy are two types of bone marrow tests that are often performed together as bedside procedures. Both are procedures used to remove a sample of bone marrow tissue or cells for testing. In the diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), these two tests are used together to confirm the diagnosis. They are commonly done at the same time to provide complementary information: the aspirate collects bone marrow cells, while the biopsy removes a small core of bone marrow tissue. Together, they allow for a comprehensive evaluation of the bone marrow.
  - Source: ACUTE MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Description: Bone marrow aspirate is used to confirm a diagnosis of AML.
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A bone marrow aspirate is a diagnostic test used to confirm AML.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Description: NCCN patient guidelines describe bone marrow aspirate as a diagnostic procedure used for AML evaluation.
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: DIAGNOSIS
  - Description: Bone marrow aspirate is used to confirm a diagnosis of AML.
  - Source: BONE MARROW TESTS
  - Target: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Description: Bone marrow aspirate is one type of bone marrow test.
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: FLOW CYTOMETRY
  - Description: Flow cytometry may be used on cells from a bone marrow aspirate sample.
  - Source: BONE MARROW ASPIRATE
  - Target: ASPIRATE
  - Description: A bone marrow aspirate is a type of aspirate that removes a sample of tissue or cells for testing.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS">
      <data key="d0">GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Genetic and biomarker tests are laboratory tests used in the management of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These tests are performed to learn about the specific subtype of AML a patient has, to identify targeted treatment options, and to help determine prognosis. By analyzing genetic and biomarker information, healthcare providers can better understand the characteristics of the disease and tailor therapeutic strategies accordingly.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS
  - Target: GENETIC TESTS
  - Description: Genetic and biomarker tests include genetic tests to learn about the AML subtype</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TUMOR STAGING">
      <data key="d0">TUMOR STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">TUMOR STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">TUMOR STAGING is the process of determining how advanced cancer is and whether it has spread to other parts of the body. It involves combining T, N, and M scores derived from imaging scans, biopsies, and blood tests to describe the extent of cancer in the body, including the size and spread of the tumor. This comprehensive assessment helps characterize the overall cancer burden and progression.
Relationships:
  - Source: METASTASIS
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Tumor staging uses the M category to indicate whether cancer has metastasized outside the pelvis
  - Source: BIOPSY
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Tumor staging is based on imaging scans, biopsies, and blood tests
  - Source: IMAGING
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Imaging and scans provide information used in tumor staging for prostate cancer
  - Source: TUMOR STAGING
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: Tumor staging is used to determine how advanced prostate cancer is
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Diagnostic tests provide information used for tumor staging in prostate cancer
  - Source: ADVANCED-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
  - Target: TUMOR STAGING
  - Description: Tumor staging identifies whether prostate cancer has reached the advanced stage</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LUMBAR PUNCTURE">
      <data key="d0">LUMBAR PUNCTURE</data>
      <data key="d1">LUMBAR PUNCTURE</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A lumbar puncture (LP), also commonly referred to as a spinal tap, is a medical procedure in which a needle is inserted into the lower back to remove and collect cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). This procedure is often performed when involvement of the central nervous system is suspected, such as when testing for leukemia cells that may have spread to the cerebrospinal fluid. The collected fluid is then analyzed to aid in diagnosis or treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Target: SPINAL TAP
  - Description: A lumbar puncture, also known as a spinal tap, is a medical procedure. Both names—lumbar puncture and spinal tap—refer to the same diagnostic and therapeutic technique. The procedure involves inserting a needle into the lower back to access the subarachnoid space in order to collect cerebrospinal fluid for analysis, measure pressure, or administer medication. It is performed by a trained healthcare professional and is commonly used to help diagnose conditions affecting the central nervous system, such as infections, inflammation, or bleeding.
  - Source: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Target: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Description: A lumbar puncture is a diagnostic test that checks spinal fluid for leukemia cells.
  - Source: ACUTE MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Description: Lumbar puncture may be needed because leukemia, including AML, can travel to the fluid surrounding the spine or brain.
  - Source: LEUKEMIA
  - Target: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Description: Lumbar puncture removes spinal fluid to test for leukemia cells in the spinal fluid.
  - Source: LEUKEMIC MENINGITIS
  - Target: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Description: A lumbar puncture may be used to test spinal fluid for leukemia cells and help evaluate leukemic meningitis.
  - Source: BRAIN MRI WITH CONTRAST
  - Target: LUMBAR PUNCTURE
  - Description: Both are used when central nervous system involvement such as leukemic meningitis is suspected.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INSURANCE">
      <data key="d0">INSURANCE</data>
      <data key="d1">INSURANCE</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Insurance is a payer that may or may not cover diagnostic tests, depending on the specific policy and circumstances. In the context of cancer care, insurance is typically billed for standard care received during a clinical trial, and it often covers such standard treatments. However, the patient remains responsible for copays and any costs not covered by the insurance plan.
Relationships: None listed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="GENETIC TESTS">
      <data key="d0">GENETIC TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">GENETIC TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">GENETIC TESTS are diagnostic tools that analyze genetic material, such as DNA or RNA, to detect gene mutations and other genetic changes. These tests serve multiple critical roles in medicine, particularly in oncology. They are used to characterize cancer, helping to identify the specific genetic alterations driving a malignancy, and to guide treatment decisions by matching patients with targeted therapies or other personalized approaches. In the context of prostate cancer, GENETIC TESTS are specifically employed to identify inherited genetic mutations that can increase an individual’s risk of developing the disease. This information can inform screening strategies, preventive measures, and risk assessment for patients and their families. Additionally, GENETIC TESTS play a key role in the diagnosis and treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where they detect gene mutations or other genetic changes that help classify the disease, predict prognosis, and guide the selection of appropriate therapies. Overall, GENETIC TESTS are versatile and essential tools in modern cancer care, encompassing both germline testing for hereditary risk and somatic testing for tumor-specific alterations.
Relationships:
  - Source: GENETIC TESTS
  - Target: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: People with certain inherited genetic mutations are at greater risk for prostate cancer, and genetic tests can identify these mutations
  - Source: GENETIC TESTS
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Genetic tests can help guide treatment by characterizing the cancer
  - Source: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS
  - Target: GENETIC TESTS
  - Description: DIAGNOSTIC TESTS encompass a broad category of medical assessments used to identify the presence or cause of a condition. Within this category, GENETIC TESTS are included as a specific subset. GENETIC TESTS are diagnostic tests that look for inherited mutations related to prostate cancer risk. Together, these entities describe the relationship between the broader diagnostic category and the specialized genetic testing used to assess inherited prostate cancer susceptibility.
  - Source: GENETIC AND BIOMARKER TESTS
  - Target: GENETIC TESTS
  - Description: Genetic and biomarker tests include genetic tests to learn about the AML subtype</data>
    </node>
