    <node id="SMOKING">
      <data key="d0">SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d1">SMOKING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Smoking is a behavior involving the use of tobacco, and it is recognized as a significant lifestyle-related factor that increases the risk of several types of cancer, including anal cancer and bladder cancer. Beyond its role in cancer development, smoking—or having a history of smoking—also negatively affects cancer treatment outcomes. It can make cancer treatment less effective and increases the risk of surgical side effects. However, quitting smoking can lead to better cancer treatment outcomes, highlighting the importance of smoking cessation for improved prognosis and overall health.
Relationships:
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Description: The text advises people who smoke to seek help to quit
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: VAPING
  - Description: Smoking and vaping are both routes of nicotine use and are discussed together in the text. The text groups smoking and vaping together as behaviors to quit because they can affect cancer treatment.
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER TREATMENT
  - Description: Smoking can limit how well cancer treatment works
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: NICOTINE WITHDRAWAL
  - Description: Nicotine withdrawal is challenging for most people who smoke.
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Smoking increases the chances of side effects during and after surgery
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: BLADDER CANCER
  - Description: Smoking increases the risk of bladder cancer and makes death from bladder cancer more likely
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: Quitting smoking can lead to better cervical cancer treatment outcomes, so smoking is related to cervical cancer management
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Smoking increases the risk of developing cancer
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: ANAL CANCER
  - Description: Smoking is listed as a risk factor for anal cancer
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: QUITTING SMOKING
  - Description: Quitting smoking is the act of stopping smoking
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: SMOKEFREE.GOV
  - Description: SmokeFree.gov is designed for people who smoke and want support quitting
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: BETOBACCOFREE.GOV
  - Description: BeTobaccoFree.gov is a tobacco cessation resource related to smoking
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: CDC.GOV/TOBACCO
  - Description: CDC.gov/tobacco provides information about smoking and tobacco use
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Smoking greatly increases the chance of side effects during and after surgery.
  - Source: SMOKING
  - Target: SMOKING OR VAPING
  - Description: Smoking and vaping can limit wound healing and greatly increase side effects during and after surgery</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
