    <node id="IMAGING">
      <data key="d0">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">IMAGING refers to the use of diagnostic tests and scans to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body for cancer evaluation. It includes a wide range of modalities such as X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), PET-CT, ultrasound (including transvaginal and endorectal), mammography, and bone scans. Imaging is interpreted by a radiologist and helps reveal a tumor's location, size, and characteristics, determine whether cancer has spread beyond the primary site, guide treatment decisions, and monitor for recurrence or response to therapy.

In initial cancer staging, imaging is used to determine the extent of disease before treatment. For cervical cancer, initial imaging may include CT, MRI, PET, and/or transvaginal ultrasound. For prostate cancer, imaging tests are used alongside PSA tests and digital rectal exams to provide a more complete picture of the prostate and help decide whether a biopsy is needed. Imaging also helps determine whether prostate cancer has spread. For colon cancer, CT with contrast is the main test used to assess the extent of disease in the body. In primary CNS lymphoma, MRI or CT scans are used to detect tumors and aid diagnosis. In some cases, imaging is also used to evaluate basal cell skin cancer.

After treatment, imaging plays a key role in evaluating symptoms or suspected recurrence. For cervical cancer, post-treatment imaging may include CT, MRI, and/or PET/CT scans, typically from the neck to the midthigh, if recurrence or spread is suspected. However, routine radiologic imaging is generally not needed at regular intervals after cervical cancer treatment unless there is a clinical suspicion of recurrence. In contrast, for stage II and III anal cancers in complete remission, radiologic imaging such as CT or MRI may be recommended every year for three years to check for spread. For rectal cancer after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance imaging with endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI with contrast is recommended to check for recurrence. After treatment for recurrence, yearly imaging using CT and/or MRI may be performed.

Imaging results are often kept in a medical binder, and patients are advised to obtain copies of their imaging results for their records. Although the specific imaging modality and schedule vary by cancer type and clinical context, the overall purpose of imaging is to provide essential anatomical and functional information that guides cancer diagnosis, staging, and management.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: IMAGING
Description: IMAGING refers to the use of diagnostic tests and scans to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body for cancer evaluation. It includes a wide range of modalities such as X-rays, computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), positron emission tomography (PET), PET-CT, ultrasound (including transvaginal and endorectal), mammography, and bone scans. Imaging is interpreted by a radiologist and helps reveal a tumor's location, size, and characteristics, determine whether cancer has spread beyond the primary site, guide treatment decisions, and monitor for recurrence or response to therapy.

In initial cancer staging, imaging is used to determine the extent of disease before treatment. For cervical cancer, initial imaging may include CT, MRI, PET, and/or transvaginal ultrasound. For prostate cancer, imaging tests are used alongside PSA tests and digital rectal exams to provide a more complete picture of the prostate and help decide whether a biopsy is needed. Imaging also helps determine whether prostate cancer has spread. For colon cancer, CT with contrast is the main test used to assess the extent of disease in the body. In primary CNS lymphoma, MRI or CT scans are used to detect tumors and aid diagnosis. In some cases, imaging is also used to evaluate basal cell skin cancer.

After treatment, imaging plays a key role in evaluating symptoms or suspected recurrence. For cervical cancer, post-treatment imaging may include CT, MRI, and/or PET/CT scans, typically from the neck to the midthigh, if recurrence or spread is suspected. However, routine radiologic imaging is generally not needed at regular intervals after cervical cancer treatment unless there is a clinical suspicion of recurrence. In contrast, for stage II and III anal cancers in complete remission, radiologic imaging such as CT or MRI may be recommended every year for three years to check for spread. For rectal cancer after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance imaging with endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI with contrast is recommended to check for recurrence. After treatment for recurrence, yearly imaging using CT and/or MRI may be performed.

Imaging results are often kept in a medical binder, and patients are advised to obtain copies of their imaging results for their records. Although the specific imaging modality and schedule vary by cancer type and clinical context, the overall purpose of imaging is to provide essential anatomical and functional information that guides cancer diagnosis, staging, and management.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-RAY">
      <data key="d0">X-RAY</data>
      <data key="d1">X-RAY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">X-ray is a form of medical imaging that uses small amounts of radiation to create pictures of the inside of the body, also known as a radiograph. As an imaging technique, it relies on radiation to visualize internal structures and is commonly employed in a variety of examinations, including CT scans, chest imaging, and pyelograms. A CT scan is described as a more detailed kind of x-ray, providing greater cross-sectional detail than a standard x-ray. A chest x-ray is a specific x-ray examination focused on the chest area. While x-rays use radiation, other imaging modalities such as MRI do not use radiation. Overall, x-ray is a foundational imaging method that uses controlled, low-dose radiation for diagnostic purposes, with CT as a more advanced application and chest x-ray as a targeted exam.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
