    <node id="RADIOLOGIST">
      <data key="d0">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d1">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A radiologist is a physician (doctor) who specializes in interpreting medical imaging tests. They are experts in reading and analyzing images from a wide range of modalities, including MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, and bone scans, as well as diagnostic mammograms. In some settings, a radiologist evaluates diagnostic mammograms while the patient waits. They study the images carefully, write a detailed report of their findings, and send the results to the patient's doctor, health care provider, or care team—such as an oncologist. Some radiologists have particular expertise in imaging the nervous system, reviewing MRI or CT images of the brain and spine and conveying results to the care team. Overall, the radiologist serves as a diagnostic expert who bridges imaging technology and clinical care, ensuring that referring physicians receive accurate interpretations to guide patient treatment.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: RADIOLOGIST
Description: A radiologist is a physician (doctor) who specializes in interpreting medical imaging tests. They are experts in reading and analyzing images from a wide range of modalities, including MRI, CT, PET, ultrasound, and bone scans, as well as diagnostic mammograms. In some settings, a radiologist evaluates diagnostic mammograms while the patient waits. They study the images carefully, write a detailed report of their findings, and send the results to the patient's doctor, health care provider, or care team—such as an oncologist. Some radiologists have particular expertise in imaging the nervous system, reviewing MRI or CT images of the brain and spine and conveying results to the care team. Overall, the radiologist serves as a diagnostic expert who bridges imaging technology and clinical care, ensuring that referring physicians receive accurate interpretations to guide patient treatment.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI">
      <data key="d0">MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">MRI</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is a noninvasive imaging test that uses a powerful magnetic field, radio waves, and a computer to create detailed pictures of areas inside the body. Unlike CT scans, it does not use x-rays or ionizing radiation. The MRI machine is typically a large, enclosed tunnel, though open MRI systems are also available. A contrast agent (dye) may be injected to make tissues, organs, and blood vessels show up more clearly. MRI can be combined with PET imaging for additional diagnostic information.

MRI is widely used to evaluate soft tissues throughout the body. It is the most useful test for identifying a glioma and is used to visualize the brain and central nervous system, including for diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma. It is also used for breast imaging, where images are interpreted by a breast diagnostic radiologist, and for spine imaging. MRI can evaluate the bladder and urinary tract to check for cancer spread, and it may be used when ultrasound images are unclear. In rectal cancer, pelvic MRI shows how deeply the tumor has grown into the rectal wall and whether it has spread to nearby lymph nodes.

In oncology, MRI plays a key role in cervical cancer management. It is used as an initial imaging test to determine the extent of disease before treatment, with and without contrast to examine the primary tumor and neck. It is also used for pelvic scanning after fertility-sparing treatment or as additional follow-up for stages 2–4A cervical cancer. MRI of the abdomen and pelvis with contrast can serve as an alternative to CT in surveillance. Additionally, MRI can be used to guide a biopsy.

While MRI is highly detailed and avoids radiation, it may be unsuitable for people with certain pacemakers, heart monitors, or surgical clips due to the strong magnetic field. Scan times are generally longer than CT. Overall, MRI is a versatile and powerful diagnostic tool for imaging the brain, spine, breast, pelvis, abdomen, and other soft-tissue structures across many cancer types and neurological conditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING TEST
  - Target: MRI
  - Description: An MRI is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
