    <node id="ULTRASOUND">
      <data key="d0">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d1">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Ultrasound (US) is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses high-energy sound waves to create real-time pictures of the inside of the body. It is a type of imaging test that is painless and does not use x-rays. During the procedure, a wand-like probe (transducer) is moved over the skin, typically with gel, to capture images of internal organs and structures. An echocardiogram is a specific type of ultrasound used to image the heart.

Ultrasound is a versatile imaging method with many applications. It is used to evaluate breast tissue and breast cancer. In gynecology, it makes pictures of the ovaries, fallopian tubes, uterus, and nearby tissues, and is often the first imaging test used to look for ovarian cancer. For prostate evaluation, transrectal ultrasound is commonly used to guide a biopsy and can help detect prostate cancer. In liver cancer screening, ultrasound is recommended every 6 months. It is also valuable for guiding biopsies; for example, it may be used to guide a lymph node biopsy, especially in the neck, where it is good at showing small areas of cancer in lymph nodes and can guide a fine-needle biopsy. Overall, ultrasound is a safe, non-invasive imaging method that provides important diagnostic information and supports biopsy guidance across many medical conditions.
Relationships:
  - Source: IMAGING TEST
  - Target: ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Ultrasound is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
