    <node id="STAGING">
      <data key="d0">STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">STAGING is the systematic process of determining the extent of cancer in the body, typically performed before treatment to guide prognosis and therapeutic decisions. It involves measuring how far a cancer has grown into the affected organ or tissue and whether it has spread to nearby lymph nodes or distant sites. Staging is a fundamental section in patient guides for various cancers, including anal cancer, and is used across multiple cancer types with stage classifications generally ranging from 0 (early, localized disease) to 4 (advanced, metastatic disease), though some systems use Roman numerals (I–IV).

The specific staging system and criteria vary depending on the cancer type. For cervical cancer, staging uses the FIGO (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics) system, which is the standard framework for rating the extent of the disease before treatment. The FIGO system is also referenced generically as a staging method for determining cancer extent. For colon cancer, staging employs the AJCC TNM system, which combines three key components: T (tumor invasion into the bowel wall), N (lymph node involvement), and M (presence of distant metastasis). These components are integrated to assign an overall stage of 0, I, II, III, or IV. For bladder cancer, staging is described as the process of measuring how far the cancer has grown into the bladder wall and whether it has spread, with stages ranging from 0 to 4.

While the overarching concept of staging is consistent—defining disease extent and spread—the operational details, including staging systems and specific stage definitions, are tailored to each cancer type. Thus, STAGING serves as a universal oncologic tool that is implemented through cancer-specific classification systems, such as FIGO for gynecologic cancers and AJCC TNM for colon and many other solid tumors. This ensures that clinicians and patients have a standardized, evidence-based language for describing cancer progression, facilitating treatment planning and communication across healthcare teams. In summary, staging is not a single uniform procedure but a framework adapted to the anatomy and behavior of each cancer, with all approaches ultimately aiming to quantify tumor burden and spread.
Relationships:
  - None listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
