    <node id="WHILE THE OVERARCHING CONCEPT OF STAGING IS CONSISTENT—DEFINING DISEASE EXTENT AND SPREAD—THE OPERATIONAL DETAILS, INCLUDING STAGING SYSTEMS AND SPECIFIC STAGE DEFINITIONS, ARE TAILORED TO EACH CANCER TYPE. THUS, STAGING SERVES AS A UNIVERSAL ONCOLOGIC TOOL THAT IS IMPLEMENTED THROUGH CANCER-SPECIFIC CLASSIFICATION SYSTEMS, SUCH AS FIGO FOR GYNECOLOGIC CANCERS AND AJCC TNM FOR COLON AND MANY OTHER SOLID TUMORS. THIS ENSURES THAT CLINICIANS AND PATIENTS HAVE A STANDARDIZED, EVIDENCE-BASED LANGUAGE FOR DESCRIBING CANCER PROGRESSION, FACILITATING TREATMENT PLANNING AND COMMUNICATION ACROSS HEALTHCARE TEAMS. IN SUMMARY, STAGING IS NOT A SINGLE UNIFORM PROCEDURE BUT A FRAMEWORK ADAPTED TO THE ANATOMY AND BEHAVIOR OF EACH CANCER, WITH ALL APPROACHES ULTIMATELY AIMING TO QUANTIFY TUMOR BURDEN AND SPREAD.">
