  - Target: TREATMENT TEAM
  - Description: A treatment team works with the patient to choose the best treatments for advanced-stage prostate cancer. 

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses medicines or drugs to kill cancer cells, primarily by damaging and destroying rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. It is most often a systemic therapy, meaning the drugs travel through the bloodstream to reach cells everywhere in the body, but it can also be delivered locally—for example, applied directly to the skin as a topical cream (such as 5-fluorouracil), delivered into the bladder (intravesically), or given by mouth, injection, or intravenous infusion. Chemotherapy is typically given in cycles of treatment days followed by rest days, and it may be used as a single agent or as a multi-agent regimen. It can be administered before surgery (neoadjuvant therapy) to shrink tumors, after surgery (adjuvant therapy) to eliminate remaining cancer cells, or as the primary treatment. When combined with radiation, it is called chemoradiation; it may also be given after chemoradiation to kill lingering cancer cells.

Chemotherapy is the standard-of-care systemic treatment for acute myeloid leukemia (AML), where it kills fast-dividing cells and is often multi-agent and given in cycles. It is also a main treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, with methotrexate being the principal drug; it can be combined with targeted therapy, and high-dose chemotherapy may be part of a stem cell rescue. For cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) that has spread, chemotherapy may be used when surgery and radiation are not possible or unsuccessful. In colorectal cancer, chemotherapy is recommended after colectomy for all stage 3 and some stage 2 colon cancers, and it is useful for pMMR/MSS cancers when given first; rectal cancer regimens may include oxaliplatin or irinotecan. For ovarian cancer, chemotherapy may be given first when surgery is not initially possible (with preferred regimens including paclitaxel and carboplatin), or after surgery to address residual disease. For cervical cancer, chemotherapy is combined with radiation as chemoradiation, often given once weekly. For anal cancer, chemotherapy is used together with radiation, and also for cancer that returns to lymph nodes or becomes metastatic. For bladder cancer, chemotherapy is one of the main intravesical therapies (delivered directly into the bladder), and may also be given systemically before or after surgery.

Side effects of chemotherapy can include nausea, vomiting, fatigue, fever, infection, tingling or numbness, rash, and diarrhea. It can also temporarily or permanently affect fertility, depending on the patient's age, treatment type, dose, and duration; therefore, fertility preservation may need to be considered before starting treatment. A medical oncologist is the specialist who treats cancer with chemotherapy. If chemotherapy is planned as the first treatment, a biopsy is typically performed before therapy begins. Common chemotherapy drugs include platinum-based agents such as cisplatin and carboplatin, as well as 5-FU, mitomycin, capecitabine, oxaliplatin, irinotecan, and methotrexate.
**Relationships:** None listed in the provided data

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEMORADIATION">
      <data key="d0">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Chemoradiation is a cancer treatment that combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically given during the same time period. It is recommended for most non-metastatic anal cancers, including as the primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer, and for nonmetastatic perianal cancers that do not meet the criteria for local excision. It is also a treatment option for bladder cancer, a possible next treatment for stage 2 or 3 rectal cancer, and an established treatment strategy for cervical cancer.

For locally advanced cervical cancer, chemoradiation consists of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) given concurrently with chemotherapy, usually platinum-based chemotherapy. This approach is recommended for locally advanced cervical cancer and for cancer found in lymph nodes or tissues outside the cervix. A typical schedule includes 5 EBRT sessions per week for 5 to 6 weeks, along with weekly chemotherapy.

More broadly, chemoradiation is used for locally advanced cancers and may be offered as a second option after local excision when additional treatment is needed. It may also be given after certain chemotherapy regimens. After completing chemoradiation, initial follow-up evaluation and surveillance are recommended. For anal cancer specifically, a digital rectal examination (DRE) is recommended 8 to 12 weeks after finishing treatment. If chemoradiation is effective, subsequent management may include further chemotherapy or a watch-and-wait approach; if signs of cancer remain, transabdominal surgery is recommended.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells. Chemoradiation is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. In this combined regimen, chemotherapy serves as one of the core components, specifically functioning as the chemotherapy portion of the treatment. Chemoradiation may be followed by additional chemotherapy, which is given to kill any lingering cancer cells that remain after the initial combined treatment.
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.</data>
    </node>
