    <node id="BRACHYTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">BRACHYTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy in which a radioactive source is placed close to, in direct contact with, or inside the tumor or body. As an internal radiation therapy, it delivers a high radiation dose directly to the tumor while limiting exposure to surrounding healthy tissue. Brachytherapy is commonly used in the treatment of cervical cancer, including as an internal radiation treatment after surgery or for pelvic recurrence. For locally advanced cervical cancer, it is recommended in combination with chemoradiation.
Relationships: (No explicit relationships listed in the data for this entity beyond its definition)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CONE BIOPSY">
      <data key="d0">CONE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d1">CONE BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Cone biopsy, also known as cervical conization, is a surgical procedure that removes a cone-shaped portion of the cervix. This removed tissue typically includes the transformation zone and encompasses both the ectocervix and the endocervix. The procedure serves a dual purpose: it can be used as a diagnostic test to gather more information after an abnormal cervical biopsy, and it can also act as a therapeutic treatment for early-stage cervical cancer. For some small or early-stage cervical cancers, cone biopsy may be the only treatment needed, as it can completely remove the abnormal tissue.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: STAGE 1A1 CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: Cone biopsy is recommended for stage 1A1 cervical cancer without LVSI
  - Source: CERVICAL BIOPSY
  - Target: CONE BIOPSY
  - Description: Cone biopsy may be used after cervical biopsy to gather more information about the extent of cancer.
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: COLD KNIFE CONIZATION (CKC)
  - Description: Cold knife conization is a surgical technique commonly used to perform cone biopsy.
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: LOOP ELECTROSURGICAL EXCISION PROCEDURE (LEEP)
  - Description: LEEP may be performed instead of cold knife conization to remove cervical tissue during cone biopsy.
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: COLD KNIFE CONIZATION
  - Description: Cold knife conization is the most commonly used surgical technique to perform a cone biopsy and remove cervical tissue.
  - Source: ENDOCERVICAL CURETTAGE
  - Target: CONE BIOPSY
  - Description: After a cone biopsy removes the cone-shaped sample, endocervical curettage may be used to scrape tissue from the cervical canal.
  - Source: SURGERY
  - Target: CONE BIOPSY
  - Description: Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: CONE BIOPSY
  - Description: Cervical cancer is a disease in which malignant cells form in the tissues of the cervix. Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure that removes a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix. It serves a dual purpose in the management of cervical cancer: it is used both as a diagnostic tool to evaluate the extent of cancer and as a treatment option. Specifically, cone biopsy is recommended for some small, early-stage cervical cancers, where it can be used to remove the cancerous tissue entirely. Additionally, cone biopsy may be performed to gather more information after an initial cervical biopsy, helping to confirm the diagnosis or determine how far the cancer has spread. In summary, for select cases of early cervical cancer, cone biopsy is a valuable and recommended approach for both assessment and treatment.
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: RADICAL TRACHELECTOMY
  - Description: Radical trachelectomy is an alternative if cone biopsy does not completely remove the cancer
  - Source: LOOP ELECTROSURGICAL EXCISION PROCEDURE
  - Target: CONE BIOPSY
  - Description: LEEP is an alternative method to cold knife conization for removing cervical tissue in a cone biopsy.
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: PELVIC LYMPH NODE EVALUATION
  - Description: Pelvic lymph node evaluation is performed in addition to cone biopsy for certain stage 1 cancers
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: FERTILITY-SPARING TREATMENT
  - Description: Cone biopsy can be used as part of a fertility-sparing treatment approach
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: STAGE 1B1 CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: Cone biopsy is an option for very low-risk stage 1B1 cervical cancer
  - Source: CONE BIOPSY
  - Target: STAGE 1A2 CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: Cone biopsy is an option for very low-risk stage 1A2 cervical cancer</data>
    </node>
