    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) that arises from B-cell lymphocytes. It is one of the two main types of ALL, the other being T-ALL (T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia), and it is notably more common than T-ALL. B-ALL encompasses several distinct subtypes, including mature B-cell ALL and forms defined by genetic markers, such as BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL (also known as Philadelphia chromosome-positive ALL) and BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL. As a disease of B-cell precursors, B-ALL is characterized by the malignant proliferation of immature B cells in the bone marrow, which can interfere with normal blood cell production. Overall, B-ALL represents a major category of childhood and adult leukemia, and its classification into genetic subtypes is important for prognosis and treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA
  - Target: B-ALL
  - Description: Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is one of the two main types of ALL and originates in B-cell lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell responsible for producing antibodies. As a distinct subtype, B-ALL accounts for a significant proportion of ALL cases and is defined by the malignant transformation of early B-cell precursors. Thus, B-ALL is a major form of acute lymphoblastic leukemia, with its cellular origin in the B-cell lineage distinguishing it from the other main type of ALL.
  - Source: B-ALL
  - Target: T-ALL
  - Description: B-ALL and T-ALL are two distinct subtypes of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), a cancer of the blood and bone marrow characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes. Both subtypes share the same overarching disease category but arise from different lymphocyte lineages: B-ALL originates from B lymphocytes (B cells), while T-ALL originates from T lymphocytes (T cells). This lineage difference is fundamental to their biology, clinical presentation, and treatment approaches. In terms of frequency, B-ALL is more common than T-ALL, accounting for the majority of ALL cases. Together, these descriptions highlight that while B-ALL and T-ALL are related as subtypes of ALL, they are defined by their respective cellular origins, with B-ALL being the more prevalent form.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
