    <node id="SUPPORTIVE CARE">
      <data key="d0">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d1">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Supportive care, also called palliative care, is a type of health care focused on relieving the symptoms caused by cancer and the side effects of cancer treatment, while aiming to improve quality of life for patients at any stage of disease. It is not meant to treat or cure the cancer itself, but rather to improve comfort and overall well-being. Supportive care may include symptom relief, emotional or spiritual support, financial aid, and family counseling. It is designed to manage side effects and support the patient throughout treatment, addressing the physical, psychological, and practical challenges that can arise.

In the context of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), supportive care is described as a treatment type that focuses on managing side effects and supporting the patient throughout the course of treatment. It helps relieve symptoms of the cancer and side effects of the therapies used, allowing patients to better tolerate treatment and maintain a better quality of life. Supportive care is also listed as a treatment option for bladder cancer, although it is only mentioned in the relevant chapter and not described in further detail there.

Overall, supportive care can be given at any stage of cancer, from diagnosis through active treatment and beyond. It is an essential component of cancer management that complements treatments aimed at fighting the disease. By focusing on symptom relief and quality of life, supportive care helps patients cope with the impact of cancer and its treatment, regardless of the specific type of cancer involved.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: NCCN's patient education chapter lists supportive care as a treatment option for bladder cancer.
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care is a specialized area of healthcare focused on relieving the side effects and symptoms caused by cancer treatment, particularly chemotherapy. It is used to manage the physical and emotional challenges that may arise during treatment, helping patients maintain their quality of life. While chemotherapy is the primary treatment aimed at targeting cancer cells, supportive care works alongside it to address the adverse effects it can cause, such as nausea, fatigue, pain, and other treatment-related complications. In this way, supportive care plays a crucial complementary role in the overall management of cancer patients, ensuring that the side effects of chemotherapy are minimized and that patients are better able to tolerate and continue their treatment.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care is a type of treatment and care option, particularly used for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Its primary purpose is to help manage the side effects of the disease and its treatment, aiming to improve the patient's quality of life and overall well-being. As a form of treatment, supportive care works alongside other medical interventions to address complications, reduce symptoms, and support the patient throughout their care journey.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care is used to relieve side effects caused by radiation therapy.
  - Source: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Target: CANCER
  - Description: Supportive care aims to relieve symptoms of cancer and side effects of cancer treatment.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Immunotherapy is a form of treatment that can cause side effects such as fatigue, fever, nausea, and infection. Supportive care is used to help relieve these side effects associated with immunotherapy. It provides essential management to reduce discomfort and improve the patient's experience during treatment.
  - Source: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care is used to relieve side effects caused by targeted therapy.
  - Source: ACUTE MYELOID LEUKEMIA
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care is part of AML treatment
  - Source: STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care helps manage side effects and symptoms that arise from stem cell rescue and its high-dose chemotherapy.
  - Source: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: Multidisciplinary care teams are central to the delivery of supportive care for cancer patients. These teams are responsible for both providing supportive care services directly and for arranging access to such services as needed. In this context, supportive care encompasses the range of physical, emotional, and practical assistance that addresses the broader needs of patients beyond direct cancer treatment. The multidisciplinary care model ensures that supportive care is integrated into the patient's overall care plan, with various professionals collaborating to meet the patient's comprehensive needs. Thus, the relationship between supportive care and multidisciplinary care is one of service delivery, where the team acts as the primary mechanism for ensuring patients receive the supportive care they require.
  - Source: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: Supportive care helps manage side effects of treatment
  - Source: HIGH-DOSE CHEMOTHERAPY AND STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Target: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Description: Supportive care helps relieve side effects of cancer treatment such as high-dose chemotherapy and stem cell rescue.

---</data>
