    <node id="MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE">
      <data key="d0">MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE</data>
      <data key="d1">MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Multidisciplinary care is a coordinated healthcare approach in which doctors, specialists, and allied health providers work and communicate as a single, cohesive team. This collaborative model is centered on planning and delivering a patient’s diagnosis and treatment, with the overarching goal of helping the patient through integrated, team-based care. Rather than operating in silos, the various professionals share information and coordinate their efforts to ensure that all aspects of a patient’s condition are addressed comprehensively. By bringing together diverse clinical expertise, multidisciplinary care aims to improve the quality, safety, and effectiveness of patient management, ensuring that diagnostic and therapeutic decisions are made collectively and tailored to the individual’s needs.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team coordinates chemotherapy as part of the treatment plan.
  - Source: RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team coordinates radiation therapy as part of the treatment plan.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team discusses treatment options including clinical trials with the patient.
  - Source: IMMUNOTHERAPY
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team coordinates immunotherapy as part of the treatment plan.
  - Source: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team coordinates targeted therapy as part of the treatment plan.
  - Source: STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: A multidisciplinary team determines whether stem cell rescue is appropriate and coordinates the procedure.
  - Source: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE
  - Description: Multidisciplinary care teams are central to the delivery of supportive care for cancer patients. These teams are responsible for both providing supportive care services directly and for arranging access to such services as needed. In this context, supportive care encompasses the range of physical, emotional, and practical assistance that addresses the broader needs of patients beyond direct cancer treatment. The multidisciplinary care model ensures that supportive care is integrated into the patient’s overall care plan, with various professionals collaborating to meet the patient’s comprehensive needs. Thus, the relationship between supportive care and multidisciplinary care is one of service delivery, where the team acts as the primary mechanism for ensuring patients receive the supportive care they require.

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SIDE EFFECTS">
      <data key="d0">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d1">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">SIDE EFFECTS are unintended effects that can arise from medical treatment, most notably external beam radiation therapy (EBRT). Common adverse effects of EBRT include fatigue, skin redness and irritation, diarrhea, nausea, and frequent or painful urination. These effects may be monitored during treatment—for example, heart tests can be used to track treatment-related side effects—and supportive care is often provided to address and manage them. Together, these descriptions highlight that side effects are both a known set of physical reactions to radiation therapy and a broader concept encompassing any unintended consequences of treatment that require monitoring and supportive intervention.
Relationships:
  - Source: EXTERNAL BEAM RADIATION THERAPY
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: EBRT causes common side effects during treatment.
  - Source: TREATMENT
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: Treatments can cause side effects and side effects are a key part of treatment planning
  - Source: SUPPORTIVE CARE
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: Supportive care helps manage side effects of treatment
  - Source: RADIATION-INDUCED MENOPAUSE
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: Radiation-induced menopause is a side effect of radiation therapy.
  - Source: HEART TESTS
  - Target: SIDE EFFECTS
  - Description: Heart tests may be used to monitor treatment side effects

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFORMED CONSENT">
      <data key="d0">INFORMED CONSENT</data>
      <data key="d1">INFORMED CONSENT</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Informed consent is the process through which a research team explains the purpose, risks, and benefits of a clinical trial to a prospective participant. During this process, the participant reviews the details of the trial and subsequently signs an informed consent form before enrolling. In some cases, the research team presents this information and the participant reviews and signs the consent form as part of the enrollment procedure.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Target: INFORMED CONSENT
  - Description: A clinical trial is a research study conducted with human participants to evaluate medical, surgical, or behavioral interventions. As part of the clinical trial enrollment process, researchers provide an informed consent form to potential participants before enrollment. This informed consent document explains the study's purpose, the potential risks involved, and the anticipated benefits. By reviewing and signing the informed consent form, participants acknowledge that they understand what the clinical trial entails and voluntarily agree to take part. Both the clinical trial process and the informed consent mechanism work together to ensure that participants are fully educated about the study before making a decision to enroll. The descriptions emphasize that informed consent is a critical and integral component of clinical trial enrollment, serving as a formal communication tool that outlines essential study information in an accessible manner.

---</data>
