    <node id="URINE TESTING:">
      <data key="d0">URINE TESTING:</data>
      <data key="d1">URINE TESTING:</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> The initial step involves analyzing the urine. This includes a **urinalysis and cytology** to study the urine for diagnostic purposes [Data: Relationships (1340); Entities (636)]. Specifically, a **urine cytology** test is performed to analyze the urine for any cancer or precancerous cells [Data: Entities (638); Sources (105)]. Additionally, **urine tests for tumor markers** may be available to detect tumor cells or markers shed by tumors into the urine [Data: Entities (639); Sources (105)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CYSTOSCOPY AND BIOPSY:">
      <data key="d0">CYSTOSCOPY AND BIOPSY:</data>
      <data key="d1">CYSTOSCOPY AND BIOPSY:</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> If urine cytology finds abnormal cells, a **cystoscopy** is needed to locate cancer in the urethra or bladder [Data: Relationships (1349)]. During cystoscopy, which uses **white light** as the standard detection method [Data: Relationships (1375); Entities (651)], a urologist can view the inside of the bladder and, if suspicious areas are found, remove tiny tissue samples for a **biopsy** [Data: Sources (105); Relationships (1351)]. A similar exam, **ureteroscopy**, may be performed to examine the ureters and inner kidneys if something seems abnormal in those areas [Data: Relationships (1357); Entities (641)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMAGING TESTS:">
      <data key="d0">IMAGING TESTS:</data>
      <data key="d1">IMAGING TESTS:</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> To evaluate the entire urinary tract and determine the extent of any cancer, a **urogram** is used. This imaging test uses a contrast solution with CT or MRI [Data: Entities (652); Relationships (1378, 1380)]. A **renal ultrasound** may also be used to produce black-and-white images of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder [Data: Entities (653); Sources (106)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURGICAL INTERVENTION (TURBT):">
      <data key="d0">SURGICAL INTERVENTION (TURBT):</data>
      <data key="d1">SURGICAL INTERVENTION (TURBT):</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> If a tumor is found, the primary surgical procedure for diagnosis and treatment is **TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor)**. This procedure examines the bladder wall and removes visible tumors, also providing a tissue sample for diagnosis [Data: Entities (667); Sources (102)]. TURBT is a key component of trimodal therapy and is often followed by **intravesical therapy** (including BCG or chemotherapy) to destroy any remaining cancer cells [Data: Relationships (1481, 1482, 1483, 1529); Entities (667)].
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INVESTIGATING OTHER CAUSES:">
      <data key="d0">INVESTIGATING OTHER CAUSES:</data>
      <data key="d1">INVESTIGATING OTHER CAUSES:</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6"> It is important to note that while hematuria is the most common symptom of bladder cancer [Data: Relationships (1332)], the most common cause of blood in urine is an **infection in the urinary tract** [Data: Relationships (1333); Entities (634)]. Therefore, a health care provider should be seen to get the urine tested to rule out infection [Data: Sources (102)].

---
</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BIOMARKER TESTS">
      <data key="d0">BIOMARKER TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOMARKER TESTS</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">BIOMARKER TESTS are medical tests that detect specific biological molecules or cells, known as biomarkers, which provide valuable information about a disease. In the context of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), biomarker tests are used to identify particular biomarkers that guide AML subtype classification, inform treatment decisions, and help determine prognosis. In the context of bladder cancer, biomarker tests look for specific tumor cells, also called biomarkers, which may offer clues about the presence of bladder cancer. However, these tests are not reliable enough to be used as a standalone diagnostic tool for bladder cancer. Instead, they are sometimes employed for surveillance after treatment, particularly for patients with non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer, to monitor for recurrence. Thus, biomarker tests serve distinct but complementary roles in oncology, aiding in disease classification, therapeutic planning, and post-treatment monitoring across different cancer types.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
    </node>
