    <node id="NARROW-BAND IMAGING">
      <data key="d0">NARROW-BAND IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">NARROW-BAND IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">NARROW-BAND IMAGING is a relatively new imaging technique used during cystoscopy to improve the detection of bladder tumors. Unlike standard white-light imaging, this method enhances the visibility of tumors without the need for any dye, allowing abnormal tissue to be identified more clearly. Because it is still in the testing phase, its use is currently considered investigational, but it shows promise for detecting bladder tumors better than conventional approaches.
Relationships:
  - Source: WHITE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging may detect tumors better than standard white light cystoscopy
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: WHITE LIGHT
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging is a newer method that may detect tumors better than standard white light detection.
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging can be used during cystoscopy to improve tumor detection.
  - Source: CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Narrow-band imaging is used during cystoscopy to detect tumors more clearly
  - Source: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Target: BLUE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are relatively new imaging methods being tested for bladder tumor detection.
  - Source: BLUE LIGHT CYSTOSCOPY
  - Target: NARROW-BAND IMAGING
  - Description: Both are newer detection techniques compared with white light cystoscopy and are still in the testing phase</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PATHOLOGIST">
      <data key="d0">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d1">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">A pathologist is a physician and medical professional who specializes in examining cells and tissues removed during a biopsy to detect and diagnose disease, particularly cancer. Using a microscope, the pathologist analyzes biopsy samples to identify abnormal cells, determine the presence of cancer, and provide crucial diagnostic information. This includes establishing a cancer diagnosis, determining the stage of a cancerous tumor, and performing biomarker testing that guides treatment decisions. The pathologist carefully notes the size, shape, and type of cells in the sample, and in certain cases—such as prostate biopsies—may measure the percentage of cancer present in each tissue core. For skin biopsies, the pathologist specifically evaluates the tissue to diagnose conditions like squamous cell skin cancer and determine the exact type and subtype of cancer. After completing the analysis, the pathologist writes a detailed pathology report summarizing the findings, which serves as an essential reference for other doctors managing the patient's care. In all cases, the pathologist acts as a specialist who transforms biopsy tissue into actionable medical information, ensuring accurate diagnosis and informed treatment planning.
Relationships:
  - Source: PATHOLOGIST
  - Target: HEMATOPATHOLOGIST
  - Description: A pathologist or hematopathologist analyzes cells and tissues removed during a biopsy.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Intravesical BCG therapy is the bladder cancer treatment in which BCG is placed into the bladder, usually after TURBT; side effects and maintenance schedules are discussed.
Relationships:
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy may be used instead of intravesical BCG if BCG is unavailable or ineffective, and both may cause similar side effects.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a type of BCG therapy given directly into the bladder.
  - Source: TURBT
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is scheduled after TURBT and at intervals after the procedure.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BCG SHORTAGE
  - Description: Because of the BCG shortage, many patients cannot receive intravesical BCG therapy or receive a reduced dose.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a form of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: MAINTENANCE THERAPY
  - Description: Maintenance therapy may be continued after intravesical BCG therapy every 6 months for up to 3 years.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BCG MAINTENANCE THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy may be followed by BCG maintenance therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: CLINICAL TRIAL
  - Description: If intravesical BCG doesn't work on its own, other treatment options are available in addition to treatment in clinical trials.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY">
      <data key="d0">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Intravesical chemotherapy is chemotherapy given directly into the bladder after TURBT or as an alternative when BCG is unavailable or ineffective, to treat cancer and reduce recurrence.
Relationships:
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy may be used instead of intravesical BCG if BCG is unavailable or ineffective, and both may cause similar side effects.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy may be used if BCG therapy is unavailable or was ineffective.
  - Source: TURBT
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy can be given soon after TURBT, within 24 hours of surgery, to clean up remaining cancer cells.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: BCG SHORTAGE
  - Description: If a regular course of BCG treatment isn't available due to shortage, intravesical chemotherapy may be offered instead.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy is a type of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Systemic chemotherapy is whole-body therapy, while intravesical chemotherapy only affects the inside of the bladder; the text explicitly contrasts the two.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy is chemotherapy directed only into the bladder rather than the whole body.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: MAINTENANCE INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Maintenance intravesical chemotherapy is a continuation of intravesical chemotherapy, usually given monthly for 1 to 2 years.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY">
      <data key="d0">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d1">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Intravesical therapy is a local treatment for bladder cancer in which liquid medication is placed directly into the bladder through a catheter. The medicine is slowly instilled into the bladder, allowing it to come into contact with the inner lining and destroy cancer cells, including those that are not visible or difficult to reach through surgical means. This therapy is typically administered after a procedure called transurethral resection of a bladder tumor (TURBT), and it is often used in combination with TURBT to eliminate remaining cancer cells and help prevent new tumors from forming. While intravesical therapy is effective for treating superficial bladder cancer, it can cause side effects such as inflammation of the bladder lining, frequent urination, pain or burning during urination, blood in the urine, and infection. Overall, intravesical therapy serves as a targeted, bladder-directed treatment that reduces the risk of recurrence after surgical removal of visible tumors.
Relationships:
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a form of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy is a type of intravesical therapy.
  - Source: INTRAVESICAL THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG
  - Description: Intravesical BCG is a type of intravesical therapy.</data>
    </node>
