    <node id="ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND">
      <data key="d0">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d1">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Endorectal ultrasound, also known as EUS, is a diagnostic imaging test that uses a small probe inserted into the rectum. The probe emits sound waves to create detailed images of the pelvis, allowing physicians to determine the extent of cancer in the area. It can also be used to guide biopsy procedures. In addition to its role in initial staging and diagnosis, endorectal ultrasound serves as an important surveillance tool after treatments such as endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) and transanal local excision. For patients undergoing surveillance, the test is recommended every 3 to 6 months for the first two years, and then every 6 months through the fifth year following treatment.
Relationships:
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: PELVIC MRI
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound and pelvic MRI with contrast are alternative imaging examinations recommended during surveillance
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: BIOPSY
  - Description: EUS may be used to guide a biopsy of lymph nodes or other areas near the rectum
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: IMAGING
  - Description: Imaging may be performed using endorectal ultrasound
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: SURVEILLANCE
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is recommended during surveillance after ESD and after transanal local excision
  - Source: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Target: TRANSANAL LOCAL EXCISION
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is an imaging option in surveillance after transanal local excision
  - Source: ESD
  - Target: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is recommended at intervals during surveillance after ESD
  - Source: IMAGING TESTS
  - Target: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound is an imaging test that can show the extent of cancer in the pelvis
  - Source: MRI
  - Target: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND
  - Description: Endorectal ultrasound may be done instead of pelvic MRI when MRI cannot be used or its images are unhelpful</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
