**ENTITY: COLONOSCOPY**
Description: Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine, inspect, and view the colon and rectum. It is a key diagnostic and therapeutic tool: most colon cancers are found during colonoscopy, and polyps can be removed during the procedure, typically using a technique called polypectomy. This allows for both detection and prevention of colorectal cancer in many cases.

In addition to its diagnostic role, colonoscopy is commonly used as a surveillance examination after treatment for colorectal cancer. For patients who have undergone transanal local excision surgery, a colonoscopy is recommended 1 year after the procedure. If the results are good, the next surveillance colonoscopy is recommended in 3 years, and thereafter every 5 years. For patients with stage 1 colorectal cancer, a surveillance colonoscopy is also recommended 1 year after surgery, as part of monitoring to detect any new cancer growth. These surveillance intervals help ensure early detection of recurrent or new disease, improving long-term outcomes.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: COLONOSCOPY
  - Description: Colon cancer often forms in polyps, which are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon. Colonoscopy is the medical procedure used to view and remove these polyps, and it plays a central role in both the detection and diagnosis of colon cancer. Most colon cancers are found during a colonoscopy, as the procedure allows direct visualization of the colon and the ability to biopsy or remove suspicious tissue. In some cases, another colonoscopy may be needed to confirm a diagnosis, particularly when initial findings require further evaluation or when polyp removal was incomplete. Thus, colonoscopy serves as both a screening and diagnostic tool for colon cancer, linking polyp detection and removal to the broader process of diagnosing the disease.

**ENTITY: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS**
Description: Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is a hereditary condition and an inherited syndrome that significantly increases the risk of developing an adrenal tumor. It is also closely related to colon cancer, as it is an inherited syndrome that causes hundreds to thousands of polyps to form in the colon and rectum. These polyps can eventually lead to invasive colon cancer if left untreated. Thus, FAP is a genetic disorder characterized by the development of numerous colorectal polyps and an elevated risk for both adrenal tumors and invasive colorectal cancer.
Relationships:
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS
  - Description: Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.

