    <node id="MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING">
      <data key="d0">MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING</data>
      <data key="d1">MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Mismatch repair testing is a laboratory analysis performed on a sample of colon tumor tissue. It is used to detect the presence of mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) or high microsatellite instability (MSI-H) biomarkers. This testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer, as it provides important information about the tumor's characteristics.
Relationships:
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Target: MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY TESTING
  - Description: Mismatch repair testing and microsatellite instability testing are recommended methods for evaluating colon cancer biomarkers
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Target: COLON CANCER
  - Description: All colon cancers should be tested for mismatch repair deficiency or MSI-H.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Description: All colon cancers should be tested for mismatch repair mutations; mismatch repair testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer
  - Source: MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING
  - Target: LYNCH SYNDROME
  - Description: If colon cancer is dMMR/MSI-H, mismatch repair testing leads to testing for Lynch syndrome</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SURVIVORSHIP">
      <data key="d0">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
      <data key="d1">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Survivorship is the post-treatment phase of cancer care dedicated to supporting individuals after active treatment has ended. This phase focuses on addressing and managing long-term side effects that may persist or emerge following treatment, while also emphasizing the adoption and maintenance of healthy habits to promote overall well-being. For cervical cancer survivors specifically, survivorship involves regular surveillance testing and close monitoring for any symptoms that may indicate a recurrence of the disease. This comprehensive approach ensures that survivors receive ongoing medical attention and support as they transition from active treatment to long-term health management.
Relationships:
  - Source: SURVIVORSHIP
  - Target: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Description: After cervical cancer treatment, patients enter survivorship and undergo surveillance for recurrence.
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: SURVIVORSHIP
  - Description: Survivorship care includes watching for symptoms of cervical cancer recurrence.
  - Source: COLON CANCER
  - Target: SURVIVORSHIP
  - Description: Survivorship focuses on long-term side effects and healthy habits after colon cancer treatment</data>
    </node>
