      <data key="d3">A PSMA-PET scan is a type of PET scan that uses a tracer targeting prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA); used mostly to monitor prostate cancer recurrence and to help determine whether targeted therapy can treat metastatic prostate cancer.  
**Relationships:**  
  - Source: PET SCAN  
  - Target: PSMA-PET SCAN  
  - Description: PSMA-PET is a type of PET scan that uses a PSMA-targeting tracer  
  - Source: PSMA-PET SCAN  
  - Target: METASTASIS  
  - Description: PSMA-PET is used to assess certain cases of metastatic prostate cancer  
  - Source: PSMA-PET SCAN  
  - Target: RECURRENCE  
  - Description: PSMA-PET is mostly used to monitor prostate cancer for recurrence  
  - Source: PSMA-PET SCAN  
  - Target: TARGETED THERAPY  
  - Description: PSMA-PET is used to determine whether targeted therapy can treat certain cases of metastatic prostate cancer  

---</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FEVER">
      <data key="d0">FEVER</data>
      <data key="d1">FEVER</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">FEVER is recognized in two distinct clinical contexts. First, it serves as a B symptom of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), where a patient experiences fever without any underlying infection or other illness. Second, fever is also a common side effect of targeted therapy, which is used as a cancer treatment. In both cases, the presence of fever is clinically significant, though its cause differs: in CLL it is a disease-related symptom, while in targeted therapy it is an adverse reaction to medication.
Relationships:
  - Source: TARGETED THERAPY
  - Target: FEVER
  - Description: Fever is a common side effect of targeted therapy
  - Source: B SYMPTOMS
  - Target: FEVER
  - Description: Fever is one of the B symptoms.</data>
    </node>
