    <node id="CARE TEAM SUPPORT:** THE CARE TEAM CAN PROVIDE COUNSELING AND MEDICINES TO HELP A PATIENT QUIT SMOKING OR VAPING [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (1396)]. SPECIFICALLY, THE CARE TEAM CAN HELP A PATIENT QUIT VAPING THROUGH COUNSELING AND MEDICINES [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (1397)]. THIS INDICATES A MULTIDISCIPLINARY APPROACH INVOLVING HEALTHCARE PROFESSIONALS.">
      <data key="d0">CARE TEAM SUPPORT:** THE CARE TEAM CAN PROVIDE COUNSELING AND MEDICINES TO HELP A PATIENT QUIT SMOKING OR VAPING [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (1396)]. SPECIFICALLY, THE CARE TEAM CAN HELP A PATIENT QUIT VAPING THROUGH COUNSELING AND MEDICINES [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (1397)]. THIS INDICATES A MULTIDISCIPLINARY APPROACH INVOLVING HEALTHCARE PROFESSIONALS.</data>
      <data key="d1">CARE TEAM SUPPORT:** THE CARE TEAM CAN PROVIDE COUNSELING AND MEDICINES TO HELP A PATIENT QUIT SMOKING OR VAPING [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (1396)]. SPECIFICALLY, THE CARE TEAM CAN HELP A PATIENT QUIT VAPING THROUGH COUNSELING AND MEDICINES [DATA: RELATIONSHIPS (1397)]. THIS INDICATES A MULTIDISCIPLINARY APPROACH INVOLVING HEALTHCARE PROFESSIONALS.</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3" />
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">
**2. Online Cessation Resources:** Several online resources are recommended as support tools for quitting smoking or vaping:
- **SmokeFree.gov** is an online quit-smoking resource and support website recommended to help people stop smoking or vaping [Data: Entities (26)]. It is designed for people who smoke and want support quitting [Data: Relationships (1385)] and is also recommended as support for people who vape [Data: Relationships (1386)].
- **BeTobaccoFree.gov** is an online, tobacco-cessation support resource specifically recommended to help individuals quit tobacco use [Data: Entities (51)]. It is recommended as support for people who vape [Data: Relationships (1389)] and is listed as an online support resource to help people quit smoking [Data: Relationships (1387)].
- **CDC.gov/tobacco** is the official tobacco-related online resource of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) [Data: Entities (52)]. It is recommended as support for people who vape [Data: Relationships (1392)] and is listed as an online support resource to help people quit smoking [Data: Relationships (1390)].

These three websites are frequently listed together as trusted, evidence-based online support tools for quitting smoking or vaping [Data: Relationships (94, 95, 97)].

**3. Quitting Smoking as a Health Intervention:** Quitting smoking is described as a significant health intervention that may reduce cancer risk and improve outcomes for cancer treatment, particularly for bladder cancer. The act of quitting smoking is supported by a range of resources, including counseling, medications, and online tools [Data: Entities (662)]. The text advises people who smoke to seek help to quit [Data: Relationships (1434)] and advises people who vape to seek help to quit [Data: Relationships (1437)].

**4. Context of Cancer Treatment:** The data emphasizes that quitting smoking can make cancer treatment work better [Data: Relationships (1438)], while smoking and vaping can limit how well cancer treatment works [Data: Relationships (1432, 1436)]. Stopping smoking or vaping is described as essential to maximize the chance of cure and minimize side effects from cancer treatment [Data: Relationships (1055)]. This underscores the clinical importance of addressing nicotine withdrawal in cancer patients.
</data>
    </node>
