ENTITY: FOLFCIS
Description: A chemotherapy regimen containing 5-FU, leucovorin, and cisplatin
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: PLATINUM-BASED CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Platinum-based chemotherapy is a specific chemotherapy regimen commonly recommended for use in conjunction with radiation therapy, particularly when systemic therapy is planned as part of the treatment strategy. It is also utilized as a systemic therapy for metastatic cervical cancer, often administered as a combination regimen to enhance therapeutic effectiveness.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: PLATINUM CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Platinum chemotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that uses drugs containing the metal platinum. This category includes well-known agents such as cisplatin and carboplatin. It is commonly administered during radiation therapy, often concurrently with external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) as part of a chemoradiation regimen. This approach is specifically utilized in the treatment of cancers such as squamous cell skin cancer and cervical cancer, among others. As a systemic therapy, platinum chemotherapy works by interfering with DNA replication in rapidly dividing cancer cells, enhancing the effects of radiation when given together.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: MITOMYCIN AND CAPECITABINE
Description: Mitomycin and capecitabine is a chemotherapy regimen for anal cancer; capecitabine is taken orally and mitomycin is given intravenously, concurrently with radiation therapy.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: DDMVAC
Description: ddMVAC is the most common systemic chemotherapy combination for bladder cancer; it consists of dose-dense methotrexate, vinblastine, doxorubicin, and cisplatin.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: MITOMYCIN AND 5-FU
Description: Mitomycin and 5-FU is a chemotherapy regimen for anal cancer; 5-FU is given by continuous infusion along with mitomycin, and is used with radiation therapy.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: 5-FU AND CISPLATIN
Description: 5-FU and cisplatin is a chemotherapy regimen for anal cancer, recommended for use with radiation therapy.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: CISPLATIN INFUSION
Description: Cisplatin infusion is an intravenous chemotherapy treatment given on the first day of 5-FU infusions as part of an alternative chemotherapy regimen.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: Systemic chemotherapy is whole-body chemotherapy delivered intravenously; it differs from intravesical chemotherapy, which only affects the bladder.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: CHEMOTHERAPY CYCLE
Description: A chemotherapy cycle is a repeating period of treatment days followed by days of rest, allowing the body to recover between cycles.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: FOLFOX
Description: FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen that combines three drugs: 5-fluorouracil (5-FU), leucovorin, and oxaliplatin. It is specifically recommended for the treatment of high-risk stage 2 and stage 3 colon cancers. The standard course of FOLFOX therapy is administered over a period of 6 months. This regimen is a common and effective option in adjuvant chemotherapy for these colon cancer stages, aiming to reduce the risk of recurrence after surgery.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: RADIATION
Description: RADIATION is a cancer treatment that is often combined with chemotherapy, a combination known as chemoradiation. As a type of cancer treatment, radiation is frequently mentioned alongside chemotherapy; the specific type of treatment administered influences the side effects a patient may experience.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: HYPERTHERMIC INTRAPERITONEAL CHEMOTHERAPY
Description: A technique used during interval cytoreductive surgery for stage 3 ovarian cancer, in which warmed chemotherapy is circulated in the spaces between abdominal organs; abbreviated HIPEC
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: IMMUNE CHECKPOINT INHIBITOR THERAPY
Description: A treatment designed to activate the immune system to better fight cancer, often combined with chemotherapy when cancer expresses PD-L1.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: CHEMORADIATION
Description: Chemoradiation is a cancer treatment that combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically given during the same time period. It is recommended for most non-metastatic anal cancers, including as the primary treatment for non-metastatic anal canal cancer, and for nonmetastatic perianal cancers that do not meet the criteria for local excision. It is also a treatment option for bladder cancer, a possible next treatment for stage 2 or 3 rectal cancer, and an established treatment strategy for cervical cancer.

For locally advanced cervical cancer, chemoradiation consists of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) given concurrently with chemotherapy, usually platinum-based chemotherapy. This approach is recommended for locally advanced cervical cancer and for cancer found in lymph nodes or tissues outside the cervix. A typical schedule includes 5 EBRT sessions per week for 5 to 6 weeks, along with weekly chemotherapy.

More broadly, chemoradiation is used for locally advanced cancers and may be offered as a second option after local excision when additional treatment is needed. It may also be given after certain chemotherapy regimens. After completing chemoradiation, initial follow-up evaluation and surveillance are recommended. For anal cancer specifically, a digital rectal examination (DRE) is recommended 8 to 12 weeks after finishing treatment. If chemoradiation is effective, subsequent management may include further chemotherapy or a watch-and-wait approach; if signs of cancer remain, transabdominal surgery is recommended.
Relationships:
  - Source: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Target: CHEMORADIATION
  - Description: Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs to kill or slow the growth of cancer cells. Chemoradiation is a treatment approach that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. In this combined regimen, chemotherapy serves as one of the core components, specifically functioning as the chemotherapy portion of the treatment. Chemoradiation may be followed by additional chemotherapy, which is given to kill any lingering cancer cells that remain after the initial combined treatment.
  - Source: CHEMORADIATION
  - Target: CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy. Specifically, chemoradiation includes chemotherapy administered concurrently with radiation therapy, meaning the drug component is given at the same time as the radiation treatment. In the context of external beam radiation therapy (EBRT), chemotherapy serves as the drug component delivered concurrently with EBRT as part of the chemoradiation regimen. The available descriptions indicate that chemoradiation, chemotherapy, or both may be listed as possible next treatment options for a patient, depending on the clinical scenario. Chemotherapy, as the drug component, works in conjunction with radiation to enhance the overall treatment effect. Thus, chemoradiation represents the integration of systemic drug therapy and localized radiation, while chemotherapy itself can also be considered independently as a treatment option.

ENTITY: CAPEOX
Description: CAPEOX is a chemotherapy regimen recommended for high-risk stage 2 and stage 3 colon cancers and is typically given for 3 to 6 months depending on the setting.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)

ENTITY: EBRT
Description: EBRT, or external beam radiation therapy, is a local treatment for cancerous lesions that delivers radiation from outside the body to a targeted area, such as the pelvis or a larger field. It is commonly recommended after surgery for local or regional recurrence, often in combination with systemic therapy. Additionally, EBRT may be administered with platinum chemotherapy as part of chemoradiation, enhancing its therapeutic effect. This summary incorporates all provided descriptions, highlighting EBRT's role as a localized treatment, its use in postoperative care for recurrent disease, and its integration with systemic and chemotherapeutic approaches.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)
