    <node id="PROCTOSCOPY">
      <data key="d0">PROCTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d1">PROCTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Proctoscopy is a medical procedure that uses a proctoscope to visually examine the anus and rectum. It is often performed under anesthesia and may be needed to look for signs of cancer in the bowel. In addition to its diagnostic role, proctoscopy serves as a surveillance examination following transanal local excision. The recommended surveillance schedule includes examinations every 3 to 6 months for the first 2 years, followed by every 6 months for the next 3 years, covering a total period of 5 years after the procedure. This comprehensive approach helps monitor for recurrence or signs of cancer in the bowel.
Relationships:
  - Source: PROCTOSCOPY
  - Target: COLONOSCOPY
  - Description: Both are endoscopic surveillance procedures recommended after transanal local excision; proctoscopy is more frequent and colonoscopy is done at longer intervals</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
