      <data key="d1">EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">(No description provided in the data)
Relationships:
  - Source: PROSTATE CANCER
  - Target: EARLY-STAGE PROSTATE CANCER
  - Description: Prostate cancer is a disease that originates in the prostate gland. It can be categorized into different stages based on how far it has progressed. Early-stage prostate cancer is one such category or stage of prostate cancer. This stage is defined by the fact that the cancer has not spread beyond the prostate gland. In other words, early-stage prostate cancer remains localized within the prostate, with no evidence of invasion into surrounding tissues or distant metastasis. As a subset of prostate cancer, early-stage prostate cancer represents a less advanced form of the disease, often associated with a better prognosis and more treatment options than later stages. Thus, while prostate cancer encompasses all stages of the disease, early-stage prostate cancer specifically refers to the confined, localized phase.</data>
