    <node id="MENSTRUAL CYCLE">
      <data key="d0">MENSTRUAL CYCLE</data>
      <data key="d1">MENSTRUAL CYCLE</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">The menstrual cycle is a recurring biological process that occurs on a monthly basis. It is regulated by hormones released by the ovaries, with progesterone playing a key role in the cycle. This cycle includes periods (menstruation), which are a defining feature of the monthly biological rhythm.
Relationships:
  - Source: MENSTRUAL CYCLE
  - Target: BREAST DEVELOPMENT
  - Description: Both are hormone-influenced biological processes mentioned together in the text
  - Source: MENSTRUAL CYCLE
  - Target: PREGNANCY
  - Description: The menstrual cycle and pregnancy are interrelated biological processes in which the hormone progesterone plays a key role. The menstrual cycle is closely connected to fertility: each cycle involves the release of an egg, which travels to the uterus. If the egg is fertilized, pregnancy may develop; if it is not fertilized, a period occurs, marking the end of the cycle. Thus, pregnancy is a potential outcome of the menstrual cycle, and both are regulated by hormonal changes, including progesterone.
  - Source: MENSTRUAL CYCLE
  - Target: SEXUAL REPRODUCTION
  - Description: The ovaries release hormones that affect the menstrual cycle in the context of sexual reproduction.
  - Source: MENSTRUAL CYCLE
  - Target: PERIOD
  - Description: The text refers to the menstrual cycle as periods.</data>
    </node>
