      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">BCG therapy is a bladder cancer treatment that has been used for decades, but it has been in short supply in the United States and worldwide; it can be given intravesically and may be reduced or shortened during shortages.
Relationships:
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical BCG therapy is a type of BCG therapy given directly into the bladder.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY
  - Description: Intravesical chemotherapy may be used if BCG therapy is unavailable or was ineffective.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BCG SHORTAGE
  - Description: The BCG shortage affects the supply and use of BCG therapy.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BCG MAINTENANCE THERAPY
  - Description: BCG maintenance therapy uses continued BCG therapy.
  - Source: UNITED STATES
  - Target: BCG THERAPY
  - Description: The United States is one of the countries where BCG therapy has been in short supply for several years.
  - Source: BCG THERAPY
  - Target: BLADDER REMOVAL SURGERY
  - Description: If regular BCG treatment isn't available, bladder removal surgery may be an option for those at high risk.</data>
