    <node id="SURVEILLANCE (REPEATED FOR COMPLETENESS)">
      <data key="d0">SURVEILLANCE (REPEATED FOR COMPLETENESS)</data>
      <data key="d1">SURVEILLANCE (REPEATED FOR COMPLETENESS)</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">SURVEILLANCE is the regular, ongoing follow-up monitoring after cancer treatment, designed to detect early signs that the cancer may have returned (recurrence). It begins when there are no signs of cancer after initial treatment, and also continues after treatment for a recurrence. Surveillance care includes physical exams, pelvic exams, and a range of tests and imaging tailored to the type of cancer. For colon cancer, surveillance typically involves colonoscopy, physical exams, carcinoembryonic antigen (CEA) blood tests, and CT scans. For anal canal or perianal cancer, surveillance includes imaging, lymph node exams, digital rectal exams, and anoscopy. In specific settings, such as after endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) or transanal local excision, surveillance is recommended for at least five years and may include flexible sigmoidoscopy, proctoscopy, endorectal ultrasound, pelvic MRI, and colonoscopy. Overall, surveillance is the follow-up care process described in cancer treatment documents as essential for catching recurrence as early as possible, thereby improving outcomes.  
**Relationships:**  
  - (No direct relationships listed in the provided data for this entity)  

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
