    <node id="RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY">
      <data key="d0">RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d1">RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, cervix, and surrounding supportive tissues. Specifically, the operation removes the uterus, cervix, much of the connective tissue holding the cervix, and the top quarter to third of the vagina. It is performed through an abdominal incision. In addition to these structures, radical hysterectomy typically includes pelvic lymph node evaluation or dissection, which is important for staging and treating cervical cancer. The procedure is most commonly used for cervical cancer, and it may also be considered for recurrent cervical cancer, sometimes in combination with brachytherapy in carefully selected patients. This comprehensive approach aims to remove cancerous tissue and assess whether the cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes.
Relationships:
  - Source: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy and pelvic exenteration are both surgical treatments for recurrent cervical cancer; pelvic exenteration is more extensive.
  - Source: PELVIC LYMPH NODE DISSECTION
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy is often performed together with pelvic lymph node dissection for cervical cancer
  - Source: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy is a form of surgery
  - Source: HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy that removes more connective tissue and the upper vagina
  - Source: CERVICAL CANCER
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy is a type of hysterectomy used to treat cervical cancer
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy may be used to treat recurrent cervical cancer when radiation is not an option and the cancer can be removed.
  - Source: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Target: BRACHYTHERAPY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy and brachytherapy are typically considered together in carefully selected patients with recurrent cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
