    <node id="PELVIC EXENTERATION">
      <data key="d0">PELVIC EXENTERATION</data>
      <data key="d1">PELVIC EXENTERATION</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgery that removes multiple pelvic organs to cure cervical cancer that has recurred in the pelvis.
Relationships:
  - Source: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Target: RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY
  - Description: Radical hysterectomy and pelvic exenteration are both surgical treatments for recurrent cervical cancer; pelvic exenteration is more extensive.
  - Source: RECURRENCE
  - Target: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Description: Pelvic exenteration is a surgical treatment option for cervical cancer that returns only to the pelvis.
  - Source: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Target: BRACHYTHERAPY
  - Description: Brachytherapy may also be given after pelvic exenteration or as part of recurrence treatment.
  - Source: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Target: SYSTEMIC THERAPY
  - Description: Systemic therapy is recommended after pelvic exenteration surgery for local/regional recurrence.
  - Source: COLOSTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Description: If the rectum is removed during pelvic exenteration, a permanent colostomy may be created
  - Source: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Target: EBRT
  - Description: After pelvic exenteration, external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) is recommended along with systemic therapy.
  - Source: URINARY DIVERSION
  - Target: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Description: If the bladder is removed during pelvic exenteration, a urinary diversion is typically performed
  - Source: VAGINAL RECONSTRUCTION
  - Target: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Description: Vaginal reconstruction is often possible after pelvic exenteration
  - Source: DOUBLE-BARRELED WET COLOSTOMY
  - Target: PELVIC EXENTERATION
  - Description: If both the bowel and bladder are removed during pelvic exenteration, a double-barreled wet colostomy may be used</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
