    <node id="LYMPH NODE DISSECTION">
      <data key="d0">LYMPH NODE DISSECTION</data>
      <data key="d1">LYMPH NODE DISSECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Lymph node dissection, also known as lymphadenectomy, is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes to test for the presence of cancer and help limit its spread. In the context of cervical cancer surgery, it is performed to determine whether cancer has spread to these nodes and to aid in preventing further metastasis. Additionally, lymph node dissection may be carried out as a standalone operation when an abdominoperineal resection (APR) is not planned, specifically to remove cancerous lymph nodes. Thus, the procedure serves both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, tailored to the patient's cancer type and surgical plan.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: LYMPH NODE DISSECTION
Description: Lymph node dissection, also known as lymphadenectomy, is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes to test for the presence of cancer and help limit its spread. In the context of cervical cancer surgery, it is performed to determine whether cancer has spread to these nodes and to aid in preventing further metastasis. Additionally, lymph node dissection may be carried out as a standalone operation when an abdominoperineal resection (APR) is not planned, specifically to remove cancerous lymph nodes. Thus, the procedure serves both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes, tailored to the patient's cancer type and surgical plan.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING">
      <data key="d0">PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d1">PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING is a procedure performed before definitive treatment for cervical cancer, specifically when there is concern that the cancer has spread beyond the cervix. It involves the surgical removal and testing of pelvic and/or para-aortic lymph nodes to determine the extent of disease. This surgical evaluation guides treatment decisions by identifying whether metastasis has occurred. If cancer is found to have spread beyond the cervix, a hysterectomy is not performed, and alternative treatment approaches are pursued instead. The procedure thus serves as a critical diagnostic and staging step, enabling clinicians to tailor therapy based on the precise anatomical spread of the malignancy.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING
Description: PRE-TREATMENT SURGICAL STAGING is a procedure performed before definitive treatment for cervical cancer, specifically when there is concern that the cancer has spread beyond the cervix. It involves the surgical removal and testing of pelvic and/or para-aortic lymph nodes to determine the extent of disease. This surgical evaluation guides treatment decisions by identifying whether metastasis has occurred. If cancer is found to have spread beyond the cervix, a hysterectomy is not performed, and alternative treatment approaches are pursued instead. The procedure thus serves as a critical diagnostic and staging step, enabling clinicians to tailor therapy based on the precise anatomical spread of the malignancy.
Relationships: (None listed in the provided data)</data>
    </node>
