    <node id="MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM">
      <data key="d0">MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM</data>
      <data key="d1">MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">A multidisciplinary team (MDT) is a group of health care and psychosocial care professionals from diverse professional backgrounds who possess specialized knowledge and experience in the patient's specific type of cancer. These experts are united in their purpose of collaboratively planning and implementing the patient's treatment, ensuring a comprehensive and coordinated approach to cancer care.
Relationships:
  - Source: MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM
  - Target: CARE TEAM
  - Description: The multidisciplinary team (MDT) and the care team refer to the same group of professionals. This team is composed of both health care and psychosocial care professionals who work together in a unified manner. Their primary role is to plan and implement the patient's treatment. In essence, the care team is the multidisciplinary team, and the multidisciplinary team is the care team—two names for one collaborative unit dedicated to coordinating and delivering comprehensive patient care.
  - Source: NCCN
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM
  - Description: NCCN patient guide describes the multidisciplinary team (MDT) approach, stating that treatment decisions should involve an MDT.
  - Source: CLINICAL TRIALS
  - Target: MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM
  - Description: The MDT makes treatment decisions and can consider clinical trials as part of a patient's treatment plan.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
