      <data key="d1">RELAPSE</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Relapse is the return of primary CNS lymphoma after earlier treatment, occurring in the brain, spinal cord, or eye. It may make stem cell rescue a treatment option.
Relationships:
  - Source: RELAPSE
  - Target: PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA
  - Description: Primary CNS lymphoma often relapses in the brain, spinal cord, or eye

  - Source: RELAPSE
  - Target: TREATMENT
  - Description: Even with the best treatment, primary CNS lymphoma often relapses

  - Source: STEM CELL RESCUE
  - Target: RELAPSE
  - Description: Stem cell rescue is a treatment approach used for lymphoma when the disease has returned after an initial course of therapy. Specifically, it is indicated in the context of a relapse, meaning the lymphoma has come back following earlier treatment. The procedure is employed after the relapse occurs, serving as a subsequent line of intervention. Thus, stem cell rescue is directly associated with managing recurrent lymphoma, with the goal of addressing the disease after prior treatment has failed to provide a lasting remission.

---</data>
