      <data key="d6">: ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND may be done instead of pelvic MRI when MRI cannot be used or its images are unhelpful [Data: Relationships (2855)]. This positions it as a key tool in the staging and surveillance workflow.

The data does not provide a direct, explicit relationship between ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND and specific numeric depth of invasion measurements (e.g., "T1," "T2," "mm depth"). However, its role in showing the extent of cancer in the pelvis and guiding biopsies of lymph nodes makes it directly relevant to tumor staging parameters and lymph node assessment criteria.</data>
