    <node id="STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION">
      <data key="d0">STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d1">STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Standard surgical excision is a conventional surgical procedure used to remove skin cancer, specifically cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) and squamous cell skin cancer. The procedure involves removing the tumor along with a margin of surrounding healthy skin to ensure complete removal of cancerous tissue. This approach is recommended for treating localized CSCC. In some cases, a skin graft may be performed following the excision to repair the area where the skin was removed.
Relationships:
  - Source: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Target: SURGERY
  - Description: Standard surgical excision is a surgical procedure and a specific type of surgery used to treat squamous cell skin cancer. It is categorized under the broader entity of surgery, and it is performed to remove cancerous tissue.
  - Source: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Target: MOHS SURGERY
  - Description: Both are recommended surgical options for local CSCC, with Mohs surgery/PDEMA and standard excision also used for high-risk CSCC.
  - Source: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Target: SKIN GRAFT
  - Description: A skin graft may be done after a standard surgical excision.
  - Source: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Target: PDEMA
  - Description: Recommended types of surgery include Mohs surgery/PDEMA and standard surgical excision.
  - Source: CURETTAGE AND ELECTRODESICCATION (C&amp;E)
  - Target: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Description: Both are recommended types of surgery for local, low-risk CSCC.</data>
