    <node id="MOHS SURGERY">
      <data key="d0">MOHS SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d1">MOHS SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Mohs surgery is a specialized, precise surgical technique used primarily for the treatment of skin cancer. It is particularly recommended for local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), especially in high-risk or very high-risk cases. The procedure involves the removal of a skin tumor in a layer-by-layer fashion. After each layer is removed, the doctor examines the tissue under a microscope to check for any remaining cancer cells. This process is repeated until no more cancer can be detected, ensuring that the entire tumor is removed while sparing as much healthy tissue as possible. Because of its tissue-sparing nature and thorough microscopic verification, Mohs surgery is considered a highly effective approach for certain skin cancers. The procedure is often followed by reconstructive surgery to repair the wound and restore the appearance and function of the affected area.
Relationships:
  - Source: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Target: MOHS SURGERY
  - Description: Both are recommended surgical options for local CSCC, with Mohs surgery/PDEMA and standard excision also used for high-risk CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: MOHS SURGERY
Description: Mohs surgery is a specialized, precise surgical technique used primarily for the treatment of skin cancer. It is particularly recommended for local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), especially in high-risk or very high-risk cases. The procedure involves the removal of a skin tumor in a layer-by-layer fashion. After each layer is removed, the doctor examines the tissue under a microscope to check for any remaining cancer cells. This process is repeated until no more cancer can be detected, ensuring that the entire tumor is removed while sparing as much healthy tissue as possible. Because of its tissue-sparing nature and thorough microscopic verification, Mohs surgery is considered a highly effective approach for certain skin cancers. The procedure is often followed by reconstructive surgery to repair the wound and restore the appearance and function of the affected area.
Relationships:
  - Source: STANDARD SURGICAL EXCISION
  - Target: MOHS SURGERY
  - Description: Both are recommended surgical options for local CSCC, with Mohs surgery/PDEMA and standard excision also used for high-risk CSCC.</data>
