    <edge source="NCCN" target="SURVIVORSHIP CARE FOR CANCER-RELATED LATE AND LONG-TERM EFFECTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVIVORSHIP CARE FOR CANCER-RELATED LATE AND LONG-TERM EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11"> NCCN publishes and provides this resource at NCCN.org/patientguidelines [Data: Relationships (427)]

## Summary of Direct Connections

The ONCOLOGY NURSE role is directly connected to:

1. Clinical intervention (Systemic Therapy): Oncology nurses administer systemic therapies, including chemotherapy, as part of hands-on patient care [Data: Relationships (2030); Entities (818)]

2. Patient education and navigation (Nurse Navigator): The role is synonymous with nurse navigator, who guides patients through cancer care with education, coordination, and support [Data: Relationships (2031); Entities (818, 839)]

3. Care team membership: Oncology nurses are integral members of the multidisciplinary care team [Data: Relationships (2029); Entities (818)]

4. Genetic risk counseling: NCCN specifically recommends oncology nurses as qualified professionals to provide counseling to patients at genetic risk of skin cancer, supporting prevention and early detection [Data: Relationships (1988); Entities (818, 0)]

The source text further elaborates that oncology nurses "provide hands-on care, like giving systemic therapy, managing your care, answering questions, and helping you cope with side effects" [Data: Sources (149)]. This confirms their role spans clinical intervention delivery, patient education through answering questions, and symptom management through helping patients cope with treatment side effects.</data>
      <data key="d12">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d13">extracted</data>
    </edge>
