      <data key="d12">PATHOLOGY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CYTOLOGY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CYTOLOGY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy samples are sent for cytology to confirm cancer and determine tumor histology</data>
      <data key="d12">CYTOLOGY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If chemotherapy is planned first, a biopsy is performed to remove a sample of the tumor before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="OVARIAN CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy is a medical procedure in which a sample of tissue is taken from the body for examination, and it is required to diagnose ovarian cancer. This procedure is performed not only to confirm the presence of ovarian cancer but also to determine its specific type and grade, which are essential for guiding treatment decisions. Thus, the biopsy serves as the definitive diagnostic tool for ovarian cancer, providing critical information about the disease's characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVARIAN CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A biopsy is a medical procedure used to confirm a diagnosis of prostate cancer. It is the definitive method for diagnosing the disease, as it allows healthcare providers to obtain tissue samples from the prostate gland for pathological examination. In the context of prostate cancer, a biopsy serves to verify the presence of cancerous cells, thereby confirming or ruling out the condition. Both descriptions agree that the primary role of a biopsy is diagnostic confirmation. Therefore, when prostate cancer is suspectedbased on elevated prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels, abnormal digital rectal exam findings, or imaging resultsa biopsy is the essential next step to establish a definitive diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BIOPSY" target="TEST RESULTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d8">TEST RESULTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biopsy produces test results that help confirm cancer type</data>
      <data key="d12">TEST RESULTS</data>
    </edge>
