      <data key="d12">CT SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scan is a type of imaging test</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING TESTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="MRI SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scans and MRI scans are both medical imaging tests used to visualize internal structures of the body, but they differ in several key aspects. MRI scans take longer to perform than CT scans, making the overall duration of an MRI exam generally more time-consuming. Additionally, the types of contrast material used for CT and MRI are different; CT scans typically use iodine-based contrast agents, whereas MRI scans commonly use gadolinium-based contrast agents. These differences in contrast materials reflect the distinct imaging technologies and the specific diagnostic information each scan provides.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI SCAN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CT SCAN" target="MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT scans and MRIs are both imaging procedures used to create detailed pictures of areas inside the body, and both may involve the use of contrast dye to enhance the images. They are often considered alternative imaging options for surveillance, particularly in oncology. For example, either a CT scan of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis, or a CT scan of the chest combined with an MRI of the abdomen and pelvis, may be used depending on the clinical situation. Both modalities are commonly used to determine how far bladder cancer has spread, helping doctors assess the extent of disease. The experience of getting an MRI scan is similar to that of getting a CT scan, but the MRI generally takes longer to complete. Together, CT and MRI provide versatile and complementary tools for diagnosing and monitoring a range of medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
