    </edge>
    <edge source="BONE MARROW ASPIRATE" target="FLOW CYTOMETRY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BONE MARROW ASPIRATE</data>
      <data key="d8">FLOW CYTOMETRY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Flow cytometry may be used on cells from a bone marrow aspirate sample</data>
      <data key="d12">BONE MARROW ASPIRATE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="BONE MARROW ASPIRATE" target="BONE MARROW BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">BONE MARROW ASPIRATE</data>
      <data key="d8">BONE MARROW BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Bone marrow aspirate and bone marrow biopsy are two types of bone marrow tests that are often performed together as bedside procedures. Both are procedures used to remove a sample of bone marrow tissue or cells for testing. In the diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia (AML), these two tests are used together to confirm the diagnosis. They are commonly done at the same time to provide complementary information: the aspirate collects bone marrow cells, while the biopsy removes a small core of bone marrow tissue. Together, they allow for a comprehensive evaluation of the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d12">BONE MARROW ASPIRATE</data>
    </edge>
