    <edge source="CERVICAL CANCER" target="STAGE 1A CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 1A CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CERVICAL CANCER is a malignancy that arises in the cervix, the lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina. STAGE 1A CERVICAL CANCER is an early stage of cervical cancer. In this stage, the cancer is microscopic and has invaded the cervical tissue only to a limited depth. Specifically, the cancer is 5 mm or smaller in size, meaning the tumor measures no more than 5 millimeters in greatest dimension. This classification indicates that the cancer has not spread to nearby lymph nodes or distant sites, and it is considered a very early, highly treatable form of the disease. Stage 1A cervical cancer is typically diagnosed through biopsy or excision procedures, and treatment often involves conservative surgery such as conization or trachelectomy, with excellent prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d12">STAGE 1A CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
