    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="INFANT ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">INFANT ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical guidance through its NCCN Guidelines, which include dedicated content addressing infant acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients contain a chapter that discusses infant ALL, and the broader NCCN Guidelines also include a chapter focused on infant ALL treatment. These materials are part of the organizations efforts to offer evidence-based recommendations and patient-oriented information for this rare and challenging form of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">INFANT ALL</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NCCN FOUNDATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN FOUNDATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Foundation funds and distributes the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are developed, updated, and hosted by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network. The NCCN Foundation funds and distributes the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are developed, updated, and hosted independently by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NCCN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">NCCN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">NCCN is the abbreviated name of the National Comprehensive Cancer Network</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="UNITED STATES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">UNITED STATES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network is an alliance of leading cancer centers across the United States.</data>
      <data key="d12">UNITED STATES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">ACUTE LYMPHOBLASTIC LEUKEMIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) publishes patient care guidelines specifically for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). These NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide comprehensive information about ALL, including details on testing, treatment options, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical practice guidelines that address the treatment of B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines include a dedicated chapter on B-ALL treatment, and the NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter covering this topic. These resources are designed to help healthcare professionals and patients understand evidence-based approaches to managing B-ALL, from diagnosis through treatment planning. The NCCN thus offers comprehensive, standardized guidance on B-ALL, ensuring that both clinical practitioners and patients have access to current, expert-reviewed information on therapeutic options for this disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN publishes patient guidelines that cover diagnosis, treatment, and supportive care for ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="AML" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">AML</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) publishes patient guidelines specifically for acute myeloid leukemia (AML). These guidelines explain current care recommendations, covering the full scope of AML management, including diagnosis, testing, and treatment. The NCCN's patient-facing resources are designed to help individuals understand the standard approaches to AML care as outlined by the network's expert panel.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="T-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a professional organization that develops evidence-based guidelines for cancer care. Its NCCN Guidelines include a chapter dedicated to T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL). This chapter appears in both the patient-focused version of the guidelines, where it discusses T-ALL in an accessible manner, and in the clinical guidelines, where it provides detailed treatment recommendations. Thus, the NCCN Guidelines serve as a resource for understanding T-ALL and its management, covering both educational and therapeutic aspects of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BURKITT LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">BURKITT LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Guidelines mention Burkitt lymphoma when describing mature B-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-POSITIVE B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides clinical guidance that includes a dedicated chapter on the treatment of BCR::ABL1-positive B-ALL. This information is presented in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which specifically address management and treatment options for this subtype of acute lymphoblastic leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">BCR::ABL1-NEGATIVE OR BCR::ABL1-LIKE B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines that include a chapter on BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL, which is identified as the most common type of B-ALL. The NCCN Guidelines for Patients also contain a chapter specifically addressing treatment for BCR::ABL1-negative or BCR::ABL1-like B-ALL, offering patient-focused information on managing this condition.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">MATURE B-CELL ALL (BURKITT LEUKEMIA)</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Guidelines mention mature B-cell ALL/Burkitt leukemia as a rare subtype of B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="APL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">APL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides patient-focused guidance that covers acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). Specifically, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients include a dedicated chapter on the treatment of APL. This chapter is part of the organization's broader patient education materials, which translate the clinical NCCN Guidelines into accessible information for individuals affected by cancer. The inclusion of APL treatment in the NCCN patient guidelines reflects the network's commitment to helping patients understand standard care options for this specific leukemia subtype. Both descriptions affirm the same fact: the NCCN's patient guide contains a treatment-focused chapter on APL.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="BPDCN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">BPDCN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a professional organization that develops clinical practice guidelines for cancer care. Among its resources, the NCCN Guidelines for Patients include a dedicated chapter addressing the treatment of BPDCNblastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm, a rare and aggressive blood cancer. This chapter is part of the NCCN's patient-focused educational materials, which are designed to help patients understand their diagnosis and treatment options. The NCCN's coverage of BPDCN in its patient guidelines reflects the organization's commitment to providing up-to-date, evidence-based information for both clinicians and patients. The inclusion of this chapter ensures that individuals affected by BPDCN have accessible guidance on available therapeutic approaches, as outlined by the NCCN's expert panel. Thus, the NCCN directly supports patient education and informed decision-making for BPDCN through this specific chapter in its guidelines.</data>
      <data key="d12">BPDCN</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">NON-HODGKIN LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The NCCN Guidelines mention non-Hodgkin lymphoma when explaining Burkitt lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d12">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK" target="PFIZER INC." id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NATIONAL COMPREHENSIVE CANCER NETWORK</data>
      <data key="d8">PFIZER INC.</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Pfizer Inc. supports the availability of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, while the National Comprehensive Cancer Network independently adapts, updates, and hosts those guidelines. Pfizer Inc. supports the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which are independently adapted, updated, and hosted by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network.</data>
      <data key="d12">PFIZER INC.</data>
    </edge>
