    <edge source="SURGERY" target="TREATMENT TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The treatment team reviews prior surgery and testing results</data>
      <data key="d12">TREATMENT TEAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">SURGERY and CLINICAL TRIALS are both recognized treatment approaches in oncology, applicable to different cancer types. According to the provided descriptions, surgery is a treatment option for thyroid cancer, and clinical trials are also listed as another treatment option for the same disease. Similarly, both surgery and clinical trials are identified as treatment options for squamous cell skin cancer. Thus, these two entities are consistently presented as parallel therapeutic avenues for both thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. No contradictions exist between the descriptions. The combined understanding is that SURGERY and CLINICAL TRIALS are each considered viable treatment modalities for thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer, with clinical trials offering access to investigational or alternative approaches alongside standard surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d12">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="TURBT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">TURBT is a surgical procedure used to examine and remove tumors from the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d12">TURBT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgery and radiation therapy are two established cancer treatment modalities that can be used alone, together, or in sequence. Both are recognized treatment options for cancers such as thyroid cancer and squamous cell skin cancer. Their specific relationship depends on the clinical situation. Radiation therapy can be given before or after surgery. When given before surgery, it may help shrink a tumor before surgical removal. When given after surgery, it is often used to eliminate remaining cancer cells. In particular, radiation therapy is an option if a concerning amount of cancer remains after surgery. This makes radiation therapy an important complement to surgery, especially when surgery alone may not be sufficient. Radiation therapy is also used as an alternative to surgery. For patients who cannot undergo surgery, or who choose not to have surgery, radiation therapy can be used instead. Therefore, while surgery and radiation therapy are sometimes combined, they can also be competing options. The descriptions are consistent: radiation therapy may be used before or after surgery; it may be added after surgery if cancer remains; it is listed as another treatment option for thyroid cancer alongside surgery; and it is a treatment option for squamous cell skin cancer along with surgery. Ultimately, the choice between surgery and radiation therapyor a decision to use bothdepends on the type and stage of cancer, the patients overall health, whether surgery is feasible, and patient preference. In some cases, surgery is the primary treatment, with radiation therapy used as a follow-up or backup. In other cases, radiation therapy is the primary treatment because surgery cannot be performed or is declined. Together, surgery and radiation therapy provide flexible, potentially curative, or disease-controlling options across multiple cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="NEUROSURGEON" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROSURGEON</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The neurosurgeon performs surgery in the operating room to remove tumor tissue.</data>
      <data key="d12">NEUROSURGEON</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="PATHOLOGY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">PATHOLOGY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Samples removed during surgery are sent to pathology for examination</data>
      <data key="d12">PATHOLOGY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGERY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are two primary treatment modalities used for ovarian cancer, and a gynecologic oncologist is an expert in both. These treatments are often combined in a coordinated sequence, and the order in which they are administered depends on the extent of the cancer at diagnosis.

CHEMOTHERAPY may be given before SURGERY, in which case it is referred to as neoadjuvant therapy. This approach is typically used when the cancer has spread too extensively to be removed safely or completely by initial SURGERY. By shrinking the tumors first, neoadjuvant chemotherapy can make subsequent surgical removal more feasible and effective. Alternatively, CHEMOTHERAPY may be given after SURGERY, known as primary or adjuvant chemotherapy. This is used to eliminate any remaining cancer cells that may not have been visible or removable during the operation. Adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended after SURGERY for most stage 1 ovarian cancers, as well as for stages 2, 3, and 4.

In summary, SURGERY and CHEMOTHERAPY are both essential and complementary tools in treating ovarian cancer. Their optimal sequence is determined by disease stage and resectability: if SURGERY cannot be performed first due to extensive spread, CHEMOTHERAPY is administered before SURGERY; otherwise, SURGERY is performed first and followed by CHEMOTHERAPY. The combined expertise of a gynecologic oncologist covers both modalities, ensuring that patients receive the most appropriate and individualized treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
