    <edge source="IMAGING" target="TUMOR STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">TUMOR STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging and scans provide information used in tumor staging for prostate cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">TUMOR STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PATIENT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PATIENT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The patient should obtain copies of imaging results and keep them organized.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging is used after cervical cancer treatment to detect recurrence or spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PET-CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET-CT is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d12">PET-CT</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">IMAGING and BIOPSY are complementary procedures in the management of glioma and other cancers, though the order in which they are used depends on the clinical context. In cases where a biopsy has already confirmed disease progression, IMAGING is the next step to determine whether the cancer is locally progressive or has become metastatic. Conversely, when initial IMAGING findings suggest the presence of a glioma, a BIOPSY is then performed as the next step to confirm the diagnosis. Thus, the relationship between IMAGING and BIOPSY is bidirectional.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team uses imaging tests to locate and assess a possible brain tumor.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging encompasses various diagnostic techniques, including the CT scan. A CT scan is an imaging test that combines multiple x-ray images to create a detailed three-dimensional picture of internal structures. CT is one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) is a type of IMAGING test used to create detailed pictures of the inside of the body. As an imaging modality, MRI serves as an initial diagnostic tool and is frequently employed in oncology, particularly for cervical cancer. Specifically, MRI is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging, helping clinicians assess the extent of disease at diagnosis. It is also one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment, allowing for monitoring of patients following therapy for recurrent disease. Additionally, MRI with contrast is utilized as an imaging method for pelvic follow-up of cervical cancer, with particular importance after fertility-sparing treatment. Thus, MRI functions across multiple stages of cervical cancer care: initial evaluation/staging, post-treatment surveillance, and long-term pelvic follow-up, depending on the clinical context and whether contrast enhancement is used. As a versatile IMAGING technique, MRI provides essential anatomical and pathological information that guides diagnosis, treatment planning, and monitoring in cervical cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ultrasound is a type of imaging modality used to visualize internal structures of the body. Ultrasound is classified as a type of imaging test, a diagnostic procedure that utilizes high-frequency sound waves to generate real-time images without ionizing radiation.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BONE SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BONE SCAN</data>
