      <data key="d11">Bone scan is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d12">IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PROSTATE CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging tests and scans are used to find prostate cancer and determine whether it has spread</data>
      <data key="d12">PROSTATE CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="COLON CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">CT with contrast is the main imaging test used to determine the extent of colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="FOLLOW-UP IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">FOLLOW-UP IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Follow-up imaging is imaging performed after treatment when recurrence or spread is suspected</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="BREAST MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BREAST MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Breast MRI is a type of imaging test.</data>
      <data key="d12">BREAST MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMAGING" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Imaging tests are used to confirm primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d12">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist is an expert in analyzing images from nervous-system imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist is a medical expert who specializes in reading imaging tests and studying the images they produce. The radiologist interprets the images generated by imaging tests, using their specialized expertise to analyze and understand the visual information captured. In this role, the radiologist serves as the key professional responsible for examining imaging test results and providing accurate interpretations of what the images reveal.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="IMAGING TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist is an expert in reading imaging tests and interpreting the images they produce.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="MRI SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in reading and interpreting imaging tests, including MRI scans. The radiologist examines MRI images to provide diagnostic information that helps guide patient care.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist studies MRI images after the scan is performed.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist's interpretation of an image showing a possible glioma can lead to a biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist plays a key supportive role within the broader care team by performing and interpreting imaging tests. Once the radiologist has analyzed the images, they send the imaging results directly to the care teamspecifically to the referring doctor or physicianto ensure that the findings are communicated clearly and promptly. This transmission of results is essential for helping the care team plan next steps in patient management, as it provides the diagnostic information needed to make informed decisions about treatment and follow-up care. By delivering accurate and timely interpretations, the radiologist supports the doctor and the entire care team in delivering effective, coordinated patient care.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting diagnostic imaging. In the context of a CT scan, the radiologist reads and interprets CT images after the scan is performed. The radiologists role includes studying the resulting CT images and interpreting imaging tests such as CT scans to provide diagnostic information.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist is responsible for interpreting medical imaging and then communicating the findings to the referring doctor. This communication takes the form of a detailed report, which is sent directly to the doctor. Alongside the written report, the radiologist also sends the actual imaging results to the doctor. These imaging results, together with the report, are intended to help the doctor plan the next steps of the patient's care. Thus, the radiologist provides both the diagnostic interpretation and the supporting visual data, enabling the doctor to make informed decisions about treatment and follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="PET SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting imaging tests, including positron emission tomography (PET) scans. The radiologist reads and analyzes PET images to provide diagnostic information that helps guide patient care. PET scans are a form of nuclear medicine imaging that produce detailed images of functional processes in the body, and the radiologist plays a key role in interpreting these images to identify abnormalities and support clinical decisions.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting imaging tests, including ultrasound. In this role, the radiologist reads and interprets ultrasound images to assist in diagnosing and monitoring medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="BONE SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">BONE SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist reads and interprets bone scan images.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="FDG-PET/CT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist interprets FDG-PET/CT scans.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="HEALTH CARE PROVIDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist writes an imaging report following an examination and sends it to the patient's health care provider. This report is a formal documentation of the radiologist's findings and interpretations. The health care provider receives this imaging report to inform patient care decisions. Thus, the radiologist and the health care provider are connected through the delivery of the written imaging report, ensuring that diagnostic information reaches the provider responsible for the patient's treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEALTH CARE PROVIDER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="PET/CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">PET/CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist interprets combined PET/CT imaging studies.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">DIAGNOSTIC MAMMOGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A radiologist evaluates the diagnostic mammogram while the patient waits</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RADIOLOGIST" target="PANORAMIC DENTAL X-RAY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RADIOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">PANORAMIC DENTAL X-RAY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The radiologist interprets imaging tests such as panoramic dental x-rays.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADIOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="UROGRAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">UROGRAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A urogram can be performed using MRI to evaluate the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d12">UROGRAM</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) is a type of IMAGING test used to create detailed pictures of the inside of the body. As an imaging modality, MRI serves as an initial diagnostic tool and is frequently employed in oncology, particularly for cervical cancer. Specifically, MRI is one of the initial imaging tests used for cervical cancer staging, helping clinicians assess the extent of disease at diagnosis. It is also one of the imaging tests used in surveillance after recurrence treatment, allowing for monitoring of patients following therapy for recurrent disease. Additionally, MRI with contrast is utilized as an imaging method for pelvic follow-up of cervical cancer, with particular importance after fertility-sparing treatment. Thus, MRI functions across multiple stages of cervical cancer care: initial evaluation/staging, post-treatment surveillance, and long-term pelvic follow-up, depending on the clinical context and whether contrast enhancement is used. As a versatile IMAGING technique, MRI provides essential anatomical and pathological information that guides diagnosis, treatment planning, and monitoring in cervical cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="IMAGING TESTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is an imaging test that belongs to the broader category of imaging tests. It is specifically used to evaluate rectal cancer depth and lymph node spread. Additionally, MRI is used when ultrasound images are unclear or to look for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Endorectal ultrasound may be done instead of pelvic MRI when MRI cannot be used or its images are unhelpful</data>
      <data key="d12">ENDORECTAL ULTRASOUND</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CLOSED MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CLOSED MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Closed MRI is a type of MRI with a capsule-like design.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="OPEN MRI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">OPEN MRI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Open MRI is a type of MRI with a magnetic top and bottom.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI (magnetic resonance imaging) can play a key role in biopsy procedures. It is capable of revealing suspicious areas within the body that may require sampling by biopsy. Additionally, MRI can be used to guide a biopsy, helping to ensure that the procedure targets the identified area accurately. Thus, MRI serves both as a detection tool and as a guidance method for biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CARE TEAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CARE TEAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The care team relies on MRI scans as a key diagnostic and monitoring tool throughout the patient's journey, including during diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up care. Because the MRI procedure involves being inside an enclosed scanner, the care team should be informed if the patient feels nervous or anxious in enclosed spaces before the scan takes place. This allows the team to make appropriate accommodations and ensure the patient's comfort and safety.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="CT SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">CT SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI and CT scan are both medical imaging modalities used to visualize internal structures for diagnostic purposes. They are similar in that each may involve the use of a contrast agent to enhance image quality, but the contrast materials used for MRI and CT scan are different. An MRI scan, while similar to a CT scan, generally takes longer and uses a more enclosed machine, which can feel more confining to patients. CT scans are often employed as an alternative when MRI is unavailable or is not recommended for a particular patient. Conversely, MRI may be used to investigate cancer in the abdomen, especially in the liver, when a patient cannot receive the contrast agent typically used for a CT scan. In such cases, MRI offers a viable diagnostic option with a contrast agent that the patient can tolerate. Overall, the two tests complement each other: CT scans provide a quicker and more accessible imaging method, while MRI offers detailed imaging with a different contrast agent profile, making it useful when CT contrast is contraindicated or when MRI is otherwise preferable.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PET SCAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">PET SCAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">PET scans may be combined with MRI for imaging.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="SURGERY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">SURGERY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If surgery is needed or being considered, MRI is ordered to evaluate the depth and spread of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">MRI is an imaging test used to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d12">PRIMARY CNS LYMPHOMA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MRI" target="ULTRASOUND" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MRI</data>
      <data key="d8">ULTRASOUND</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
