      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Follow-up imaging is part of surveillance after treatment</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="PHYSICAL AND PELVIC EXAMS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL AND PELVIC EXAMS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance includes regular physical and pelvic exams.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="PHYSICAL EXAM" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">PHYSICAL EXAM</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Physical exams are part of surveillance for stage 2 and 3 colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance and imaging are closely intertwined in the management of anal cancer. Imaging is a recommended component of surveillance for patients with stage II and III anal cancers who have achieved complete remission. As part of post-treatment follow-up, imaging is also recommended every year for three years during surveillance after treatment for recurrence. Additionally, imaging is employed during surveillance when new or worsening symptoms arise that suggest possible recurrence or spread of disease. The surveillance process specifically includes imaging modalities such as endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI to monitor for disease status. In summary, imaging serves as a routine, scheduled tool in surveillance protocols, as well as a targeted diagnostic measure when clinical signs raise concern, with the choice of modality typically involving endorectal ultrasound or pelvic MRI.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="CHEMORADIATION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMORADIATION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance is recommended after completing chemoradiation to check treatment results</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="RECURRENCE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance is performed to detect cancer recurrence or new cancer growth early.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="ANOSCOPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">ANOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance and anoscopy are closely related in post-treatment monitoring. Anoscopy is a recommended component of surveillance protocols when feasible. Specifically, after lymph node-only treatment, surveillance includes anoscopy performed every 6 to 12 months for a period of 3 years. Thus, the surveillance strategy incorporates regular anoscopic examinations to monitor for potential recurrence or complications, with the frequency and duration clearly defined in the context of lymph node-only treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="PROCTOSCOPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d8">PROCTOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Proctoscopy is a surveillance examination performed after transanal local excision</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURVEILLANCE" target="DIGITAL RECTAL EXAMINATION" id="related_to">
