      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Staging and grading are used together to evaluate how far and how fast cancer has grown</data>
      <data key="d12">GRADING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM SCORING" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TNM SCORING</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">TNM scores are assigned as part of cancer staging and are then combined to assign a numbered stage.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM SCORING" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TNM SCORING</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The M component of TNM scoring indicates whether the cancer has metastasized to distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d12">TNM SCORING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created and developed the cancer staging system described in the text, which is the staging system used for cancer staging. This AJCC staging system is one of the two standard ways to describe the extent of ovarian cancer. As the entity responsible for this widely used framework, the American Joint Committee on Cancer established a systematic method for categorizing the progression and spread of cancer, providing clinicians and researchers with a common language to describe tumor characteristics. The cancer staging system developed by the committee is integral to treatment planning and prognosis, and its application to ovarian cancer represents one of its key clinical uses, where it serves as a standard alongside another classification approach to define how far the disease has advanced.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="NPC STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">NPC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AJCC staging system is used in NPC staging.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="TNM SCORES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">TNM SCORES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AJCC staging system uses TNM scores to assign a cancer stage.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="TNM SCORING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">TNM SCORING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AJCC staging system uses TNM scoring to describe the extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AJCC TNM system assesses M (metastasis) to stage colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The AJCC TNM system is used to stage colon cancer, assigning stages based on T, N, and M scores.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="PATHOLOGIC STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The American Joint Committee on Cancer developed one of the staging systems used for pathologic staging of ovarian cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER" target="INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">INTERNATIONAL FEDERATION OF GYNECOLOGY AND OBSTETRICS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) and the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) have both developed staging systems for ovarian cancer. The two systems are similar in structure and intent, providing standardized frameworks for classifying the extent of the disease. Although both staging systems are recognized and used in clinical practice, the FIGO system is employed most often. This reflects its widespread acceptance and practical utility in the management and communication of ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d12">AMERICAN JOINT COMMITTEE ON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGICAL STAGING" target="HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgical staging is performed during the surgery of hysterectomy and BSO when cancer has not visibly spread, taking samples to check for microscopic metastases</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGICAL STAGING" target="EXTRA IMAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">EXTRA IMAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgical staging and extra imaging are two approaches used to determine whether lymph nodes contain cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">EXTRA IMAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGICAL STAGING" target="STAGE 1 RECTAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 1 RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgical staging assigns stage 1 when the tumor has grown into the second or third layer of the rectal wall</data>
      <data key="d12">STAGE 1 RECTAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SURGICAL STAGING" target="STAGE 2 RECTAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SURGICAL STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">STAGE 2 RECTAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surgical staging assigns stage 2 when the tumor has grown through the rectal wall and possibly into nearby tissues or organs</data>
      <data key="d12">STAGE 2 RECTAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIC STAGING" target="CANCER STAGING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER STAGING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer staging is the overall process of determining the extent of cancer in the body. It is often performed as pathologic staging after surgery. Pathologic staging is the post-surgery portion of cancer staging, determined from surgical tissue.</data>
      <data key="d12">CANCER STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIC STAGING" target="HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Information gained during surgery contributes to determining the pathologic stage of ovarian cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">HYSTERECTOMY AND BSO</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="TNM SCORES" target="METASTASIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">TNM SCORES</data>
      <data key="d8">METASTASIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Metastasis is the M category in TNM scores.</data>
      <data key="d12">TNM SCORES</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NPC STAGING" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">NPC STAGING</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biomarker testing is part of nasopharyngeal carcinoma staging to see if the cancer has genes or mutations suggesting treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">NPC STAGING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NPC STAGING" target="TNM SCORES" id="related_to">
