    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Supportive care is a specialized area of healthcare focused on relieving the side effects and symptoms caused by cancer treatment, particularly chemotherapy. It is used to manage the physical and emotional challenges that may arise during treatment, helping patients maintain their quality of life. While chemotherapy is the primary treatment aimed at targeting cancer cells, supportive care works alongside it to address the adverse effects it can cause, such as nausea, fatigue, pain, and other treatment-related complications. In this way, supportive care plays a crucial complementary role in the overall management of cancer patients, ensuring that the side effects of chemotherapy are minimized and that patients are better able to tolerate and continue their treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="NAUSEA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">NAUSEA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy regimens containing irinotecan tend to cause nausea</data>
      <data key="d12">NAUSEA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CHEMOTHERAPY" target="RADIATION THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">RADIATION THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Chemotherapy and radiation therapy are two treatment modalities that are frequently combined to enhance effectiveness, particularly in oncology. When used together, the combination is referred to as chemoradiation. Chemotherapy may be administered as part of recommended treatment regimens alongside radiation therapy to treat anal cancer. Additionally, chemotherapy may be given in addition to radiation for recurrent inguinal lymph nodes, with chemoradiation combining both modalities. Radiation therapy, for its part, may be delivered alone or in combination with chemotherapy, and can also be combined with systemic therapy in certain situations. In all cases, the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapytermed chemoradiationrepresents a coordinated approach in which the two treatments complement each other, whether the goal is curative treatment for anal cancer or management of recurrent disease in inguinal lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d12">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
