      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Colectomy is needed for colon cancer not found early enough to be removed by polypectomy; it removes the cancerous part of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d12">COLECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="LYNCH SYNDROME" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Lynch syndrome is an inherited syndrome directly related to colon cancer. Individuals born with Lynch syndrome face a significantly elevated risk of developing colon cancer, making it a key hereditary factor in the disease's onset.</data>
      <data key="d12">LYNCH SYNDROME</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="ADENOMA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">ADENOMA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Adenomas can become invasive colon cancer over time</data>
      <data key="d12">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="COLONOSCOPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">COLONOSCOPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Colon cancer often forms in polyps, which are abnormal growths on the inner lining of the colon. Colonoscopy is the medical procedure used to view and remove these polyps, and it plays a central role in both the detection and diagnosis of colon cancer. Most colon cancers are found during a colonoscopy, as the procedure allows direct visualization of the colon and the ability to biopsy or remove suspicious tissue. In some cases, another colonoscopy may be needed to confirm a diagnosis, particularly when initial findings require further evaluation or when polyp removal was incomplete. Thus, colonoscopy serves as both a screening and diagnostic tool for colon cancer, linking polyp detection and removal to the broader process of diagnosing the disease.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">All colon cancers should be tested for mismatch repair mutations; mismatch repair testing is recommended for everyone diagnosed with colon cancer</data>
      <data key="d12">MISMATCH REPAIR TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">COLON CANCER</data>
      <data key="d8">FAMILIAL ADENOMATOUS POLYPOSIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an inherited syndrome closely related to colon cancer. In classic FAP, the polyps characteristic of the condition can become invasive colon cancer, often by age 50. Thus, colon cancer is a major complication of this inherited syndrome, with FAP serving as a direct precursor condition.</data>
      <data key="d12">COLON CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COLON CANCER" target="NON-METASTATIC CANCER" id="related_to">
