    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="CERVICAL CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cervical cancer can recur after treatment, and recurrence is a major focus of follow-up care.</data>
      <data key="d12">CERVICAL CANCER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BLOOD TEST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">BLOOD TEST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Blood tests may be ordered if symptoms or a suspicious physical exam suggest recurrent cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">BLOOD TEST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="BIOMARKER TESTING" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Biomarker testing plays a critical role in the management of recurrent cancers, helping to guide treatment decisions and identify potential therapeutic options. In the context of recurrent ovarian cancer, biomarker testing is used to help direct treatment by providing information that can inform which therapies are most likely to be effective for the individual patient. For recurrent cervical cancer, biomarker testing is specifically recommended for all patients with disease recurrence, as it serves to identify targeted therapy options or determine eligibility for clinical trials. Thus, across both recurrent ovarian and recurrent cervical cancers, biomarker testing is an essential tool for personalizing care after a recurrence, enabling clinicians to match patients with the most appropriate treatments, including targeted therapies, and to explore clinical trial opportunities when standard options are limited.</data>
      <data key="d12">BIOMARKER TESTING</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="SURVIVORSHIP" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Survivorship care includes watching for symptoms of cervical cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVIVORSHIP</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="COMPLETE REMISSION" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">COMPLETE REMISSION</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cancer can return after complete remission; if it returns more than 6 months later, the disease is considered platinum-sensitive.</data>
      <data key="d12">COMPLETE REMISSION</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="SURVEILLANCE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">SURVEILLANCE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Surveillance is conducted to monitor for recurrence or relapse.</data>
      <data key="d12">SURVEILLANCE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RECURRENCE</data>
      <data key="d8">RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Radical hysterectomy may be used to treat recurrent cervical cancer when radiation is not an option and the cancer can be removed.</data>
      <data key="d12">RADICAL HYSTERECTOMY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="RECURRENCE" target="PELVIC EXENTERATION" id="related_to">
