    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A drug, device, or treatment approach found safe and effective in a clinical trial may be approved by the U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d12">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="CLINICAL TRIALS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">U.S. FDA</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIALS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Clinical trials offer participants access to investigational treatments that have not yet been fully approved. Over time, these treatments may receive approval from the U.S. FDA, making them more widely available. Thus, clinical trials serve as a pathway for patients to obtain emerging therapies while also generating data that can support eventual regulatory approval by the U.S. FDA.</data>
      <data key="d12">U.S. FDA</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="U.S. FDA" target="UNITED STATES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">U.S. FDA</data>
