    <edge source="SUPPORTIVE CARE" target="CANCER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d8">CANCER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Supportive care aims to relieve symptoms of cancer and side effects of cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPPORTIVE CARE" target="MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d8">MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Multidisciplinary care teams are central to the delivery of supportive care for cancer patients. These teams are responsible for both providing supportive care services directly and for arranging access to such services as needed. In this context, supportive care encompasses the range of physical, emotional, and practical assistance that addresses the broader needs of patients beyond direct cancer treatment. The multidisciplinary care model ensures that supportive care is integrated into the patients overall care plan, with various professionals collaborating to meet the patients comprehensive needs. Thus, the relationship between supportive care and multidisciplinary care is one of service delivery, where the team acts as the primary mechanism for ensuring patients receive the supportive care they require.</data>
      <data key="d12">MULTIDISCIPLINARY CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SUPPORTIVE CARE" target="SIDE EFFECTS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d8">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Supportive care helps manage side effects of treatment</data>
      <data key="d12">SIDE EFFECTS</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMMUNOTHERAPY" target="SUPPORTIVE CARE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Immunotherapy is a form of treatment that can cause side effects such as fatigue, fever, nausea, and infection. Supportive care is used to help relieve these side effects associated with immunotherapy. It provides essential management to reduce discomfort and improve the patient's experience during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d12">SUPPORTIVE CARE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMMUNOTHERAPY" target="FATIGUE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">FATIGUE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Immunotherapy may cause fatigue</data>
      <data key="d12">FATIGUE</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="IMMUNOTHERAPY" target="TARGETED THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">IMMUNOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">TARGETED THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Both are cancer treatment approaches described together; targeted therapy attacks specific cancer-cell parts, while immunotherapy uses the immune system to fight cancer.</data>
      <data key="d12">TARGETED THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
