    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="HEMATOPATHOLOGIST" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">HEMATOPATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A pathologist or hematopathologist analyzes cells and tissues removed during a biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d12">HEMATOPATHOLOGIST</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="BONE MARROW BIOPSY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">BONE MARROW BIOPSY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A pathologist analyzes tissue removed during a biopsy, such as a bone marrow biopsy.</data>
      <data key="d12">BONE MARROW BIOPSY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="PATHOLOGIST" target="DOCTOR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">PATHOLOGIST</data>
      <data key="d8">DOCTOR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">A pathologist is a doctor who specializes in testing cells and tissue to diagnose disease. This specialist prepares a detailed pathology report based on those laboratory findings. Separately, the doctor who is caring for the patient reviews the pathology report prepared by the pathologist with the patient, explaining the results and their implications. Together, the pathologists diagnostic work and the doctors patient-facing review form a coordinated process: the pathologist identifies and interprets cellular and tissue abnormalities, while the doctor uses that information to guide the patients understanding and subsequent treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d12">DOCTOR</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical chemotherapy may be used instead of intravesical BCG if BCG is unavailable or ineffective, and both may cause similar side effects.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="BCG SHORTAGE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">BCG SHORTAGE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Because of the BCG shortage, many patients cannot receive intravesical BCG therapy or receive a reduced dose.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="MAINTENANCE THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">MAINTENANCE THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Maintenance therapy may be continued after intravesical BCG therapy every 6 months for up to 3 years.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="BCG MAINTENANCE THERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">BCG MAINTENANCE THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical BCG therapy may be followed by BCG maintenance therapy.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY" target="CLINICAL TRIAL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CLINICAL TRIAL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If intravesical BCG doesn't work on its own, other treatment options are available in addition to treatment in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL BCG THERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="BCG SHORTAGE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">BCG SHORTAGE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">If a regular course of BCG treatment isn't available due to shortage, intravesical chemotherapy may be offered instead.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">SYSTEMIC CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Systemic chemotherapy is whole-body therapy, while intravesical chemotherapy only affects the inside of the bladder; the text explicitly contrasts the two.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical chemotherapy is chemotherapy directed only into the bladder rather than the whole body.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" target="MAINTENANCE INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">MAINTENANCE INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Maintenance intravesical chemotherapy is a continuation of intravesical chemotherapy, usually given monthly for 1 to 2 years.</data>
      <data key="d12">INTRAVESICAL CHEMOTHERAPY</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="INTRAVESICAL THERAPY" target="TURBT" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">INTRAVESICAL THERAPY</data>
      <data key="d8">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Intravesical therapy and TURBT (transurethral resection of bladder tumor) are two treatments commonly used together for bladder cancer. TURBT is a surgical procedure that removes visible tumors from the bladder lining. Intravesical therapy involves delivering anticancer drugs directly into the bladder. It is often performed in combination with TURBT, and its primary purpose is to destroy cancer cells that TURBT left behind, helping to reduce the risk of recurrence and eliminate residual disease. Together, these treatments provide a combined approach: TURBT removes the main tumor mass, while intravesical therapy targets remaining microscopic cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d12">TURBT</data>
    </edge>
