ENTITY: NCCN
Description: The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit medical organization and alliance of cancer centers in the United States. It is widely known for publishing evidence-based cancer care guidelines for both clinicians and patients. Its NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology are paired with the NCCN Guidelines for Patients, which translate expert-reviewed recommendations into easy-to-understand language. These patient guidelines help individuals understand their diagnosis, prepare for conversations with their care team, and take part in treatment decisions.

NCCN makes patient resources available through multiple channels, including the NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer mobile app, the website NCCN.org/patientguidelines, and print or digital guides. The organization actively seeks patient input through online surveys and feedback pages, such as NCCN.org/patients/feedback, NCCN.org/patients/response, and NCCN.org/patients/comments. NCCN also offers a directory of NCCN Cancer Centers, helping patients locate specialized care and clinical trials across the United States.

The scope of NCCN's patient guidelines is broad, covering numerous cancer types and care topics. Specific titles include guidelines for mouth cancer, nasopharyngeal cancer, throat cancer, esophageal cancer, gastric and esophageal topics, gallbladder and bile duct tumors, liver cancer, pancreatic cancer (including pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors), colorectal cancer, colon cancer, rectal cancer, colorectal cancer screening, lung cancer (NSCLC and SCLC), breast cancer (including metastatic breast cancer), bladder cancer, kidney cancer, adrenal tumors, prostate cancer, bone cancer, soft tissue sarcoma, melanoma, squamous cell skin cancer, cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, lymphoma, primary CNS lymphoma, CLL/SLL, and ALL. In addition to disease-specific guidance, NCCN addresses supportive care and survivorship topics such as fertility preservation for adolescent and young adult cancer patients, chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, distress during cancer care, anemia and neutropenia, and immunotherapy side effects from immune checkpoint inhibitors. NCCN also publishes survivorship care guides and resources covering medical appointments, care teams, medical and family history, tests, treatment planning, and follow-up care, including recommendations for how often skin exams should be performed after treatment for squamous cell skin cancer.

NCCN's experts provide clinical recommendations not only for treatment but also for screening and follow-up. For example, they advise regular skin cancer screening for individuals with certain genetic syndromes or weakened immune systems, referral to specialized cancer centers, and cancer genetic counseling when appropriate. The organization also recommends that everyone with primary CNS lymphoma consider joining a clinical trial. Overall, NCCN serves as a comprehensive source of current, expert-reviewed cancer information, patient education, and clinical trial navigation, supporting both patients and providers in staying informed about best practices in cancer care. [Data: Entities (0)]

